{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TopRideSmkKy5abiv7uuyt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have enough information from the CoRL reviewer guidelines to proceed. Based on my research:\n\nCoRL uses a review form that evaluates:\n- Originality\n- Quality \n- Clarity\n- Significance\n- Relevance (to robot learning)\n- Limitations\n\nThe overall score typically follows a 1-10 scale similar to other ML conferences (via OpenReview). CoRL acceptance rate is typically around 25-35%.\n\nNow let me use the CoRL 2023/2024 guidelines I found plus standard robotics/ML conference review criteria to dispatch the two reviewers.\n\nLet me now dispatch R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) as parallel agents.\n\n\n---\n\nI've reviewed the paper thoroughly—it's a comprehensive empirical study examining how different token pruning strategies perform across five VLA models on LIBERO tasks, with three core findings around pixel-shuffle entanglement, importance metrics outperforming connector architectures, and VTR's architecture-specific recovery properties. The review context provides the experimental setup and expected outcomes I need to evaluate these claims fairly.\n\nLooking at the framework predictions now—the original 2-axis framework only achieved about 62% accuracy, which is concerning. The pixel-shuffle entanglement claim and the VTR case study appear to hold up, but the broader framework, the scaling law hypothesis, and several other predictions didn't materialize as expected. I'm going to bring in two reviewer agents to help assess this systematically.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3605,"cache_read_input_tokens":64656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3605},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}