{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DnDDBoy1fMZ8HVAA4RgxVL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BgBDWKCefVibmmAGNi7Z1u","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/docs/paper/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1110,"cache_read_input_tokens":67235,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1110,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01STXSqAQ3t5rr6naL2y5ZvK","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"8f5ba194-1e32-4ecb-abb7-7dcf962ccb37"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BgBDWKCefVibmmAGNi7Z1u","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\t\\begin{figure*}[t]\n5\t\\centering\n6\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_hero_overview.pdf}\n7\t\\caption{Overview of VLA token compressibility.\n8\t\\textbf{(a)}~The same robot observation produces qualitatively different token importance patterns through different vision connectors, quantified by the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms.\n9\t\\textbf{(b)}~Two axes govern pruning robustness: token coupling (independent vs.\\ entangling connectors) and task complexity (single-step vs.\\ multi-step).\n10\t\\textbf{(c)}~A single forward pass CV diagnostic routes each architecture to an appropriate compression strategy: uniform pruning, importance-based scoring, or learned re-encoding.}\n11\t\\label{fig:teaser}\n12\t\\end{figure*}\n13\t\n14\tRemove 5\\% of vision tokens from SmolVLA and its success rate collapses from 59.2\\% to near-zero (${\\leq}$6.6\\%) across all task suites and pruning methods. Apply the same 5\\% reduction to $\\pi_{0.5}$ and it retains 98.2\\% under L2 norm selection. Both are Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on a Vision Transformer (ViT) encoder feeding tokens through a connector into a language model backbone; the critical difference is not model scale (450M vs.\\ 4B parameters) but the connector architecture. Token pruning is well-established for ViTs~\\cite{rao2021dynamicvit,bolya2023tome,liang2022evit} and a growing body of work extends it to VLAs~\\cite{cheng2026vlaiap,xu2025vlacache,yang2025efficientvla,liu2025vlapruner}, but each method validates on a single architecture.\n15\t\n16\tThese methods implicitly assume that pruning transfers across VLA designs. Yet current VLAs employ fundamentally different vision connectors: linear projections~\\cite{black2024pi0}, MLP projectors~\\cite{kim2024openvla}, pixel-shuffle folding~\\cite{shukor2025smolvla}, and learned encoders with cross-token self-attention~\\cite{zheng2025xvla}. Each imposes distinct constraints on how visual information distributes across tokens. Deploying a pruning method validated on one connector type to another risks silent degradation or complete failure. No prior work evaluates pruning robustness across both connector architectures and task complexity.\n17\t\n18\tWe present an empirical study of VLA token compressibility, organized along two axes (Figure~\\ref{fig:teaser}). Along the coupling axis, token-independent connectors (linear projections, MLP projectors) degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors produce qualitatively different failures: SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle collapses to near-zero (${\\leq}$6.6\\%) at every retention ratio, while X-VLA's BART encoder exhibits sharp threshold drops. Along the task complexity axis, under random pruning at $k{=}0.9$, X-VLA loses just 1.4 percentage points on single-step Object but 27.8 percentage points on multi-step Goal, a $20{\\times}$ amplification driven entirely by task complexity. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms captures connector coupling on a continuous spectrum (0.038 to 0.314 across six architectures spanning all three connector families) and correlates with both pruning tolerance and method sensitivity: $\\pi_{0.5}$ exhibits a $17{\\times}$ greater method gap than X-VLA at $k{=}0.95$. Scoring effectiveness exhibits a double dissociation: attention is effective on X-VLA's entangling connector ($+$31\\,pp over random) but is ineffective to harmful on $\\pi_{0.5}$'s linear connector ($-$2 to $-$30\\,pp), while L2 norm shows the converse, effective on $\\pi_{0.5}$ ($+$35.6\\,pp at $k{=}0.85$ decaying to $+$5.6\\,pp at $k{=}0.95$) but anti-informative on X-VLA ($-$8 to $-$27\\,pp).\n19\t\n20\tFor pixel-shuffle connectors where all pruning strategies yield near-zero SR, we introduce Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module (${\\sim}$22M parameters, ${\\sim}$5\\% of SmolVLA) that recovers over 60\\% success rate at 75\\% compression through learned re-encoding.\n21\t\n22\tOur contributions are:\n23\t\\begin{itemize}\n24\t    \\item A compressibility analysis across six VLA architectures (450M to 7B) on four LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023libero} task suites, revealing connector architecture, task complexity, and scoring method as interacting determinants of pruning robustness.\n25\t    \\item CV of token L2 norms as a single-pass diagnostic that provides a coarse indicator of connector compressibility type (uniform, graded, or catastrophic) without downstream evaluation. Validated on four closed-loop architectures; we expect the trend to generalize but acknowledge the limited sample.\n26\t    \\item Showing that learned re-encoding (regardless of module architecture) recovers a pixel-shuffle VLA from 0.0\\% to over 60\\% success rate at 75\\% compression, establishing hard deletion as the failure mechanism rather than redundancy.\n27\t\\end{itemize}\n28\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01STXSqAQ3t5rr6naL2y5ZvK","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"ae78a358-ded5-4a35-af37-81054e022597","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:20:31.304Z"}
