{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013CySLSsSdESBh8YRJY6dBq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AodDMU7XRoxCVvUiz6EQ6K","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T050917/best/paper.tex","offset":340,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24808,"cache_read_input_tokens":94563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":24808,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01STXSqAQ3t5rr6naL2y5ZvK","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"18a9728e-3981-4987-ae51-b9dc3b9900e7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AodDMU7XRoxCVvUiz6EQ6K","type":"tool_result","content":"340\t\\textbf{Practitioner prescription}: Measure CV. If CV $< 0.15$: use random pruning. If CV $> 0.2$: use L2-norm pruning. If $0.15 < \\text{CV} < 0.2$: either method works at mild compression ($k \\geq 0.8$).\n341\t\n342\t\n343\t% ------------------------------------------------------------\n344\t\\subsection{Attention-Based Pruning Rescues Entangling Connectors}\n345\t\\label{sec:attention_pruning}\n346\t% ------------------------------------------------------------\n347\t\n348\tThe failure of L2-norm on entangling connectors raises the question: does a \\emph{correct} importance metric exist? We evaluate attention-based token importance on X-VLA using three aggregation strategies: last-layer attention, mean attention across layers, and attention rollout. Table~\\ref{tab:attention_pruning} presents results at $k\\!=\\!0.5$ across all four suites.\n349\t\n350\t\\begin{table}[t]\n351\t\\centering\n352\t\\caption{Attention-based pruning \\vs\\ L2-norm on X-VLA at $k\\!=\\!0.5$. Attention metrics achieve near-baseline SR across all suites, with advantages of $+$30--57\\pp\\ over L2-norm.}\n353\t\\label{tab:attention_pruning}\n354\t\\small\n355\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n356\t\\toprule\n357\tSuite & Baseline & L2norm & Random & Attn-last & Attn-mean \\\\\n358\t\\midrule\n359\tObject & 99.2 & 42.6 & 70.6 & 99.2 & 98.4 \\\\\n360\tSpatial & 93.0 & 58.8 & 71.4 & 88.8 & 91.6 \\\\\n361\tGoal & 98.4 & 59.0 & 67.2 & 98.2 & 97.4 \\\\\n362\tLong & 94.0 & 44.6 & 61.6 & 90.2 & 94.0 \\\\\n363\t\\bottomrule\n364\t\\end{tabular}\n365\t\\end{table}\n366\t\n367\tAttention-last achieves 99.2\\% on Object ($+$56.6\\pp\\ over L2-norm) and 98.2\\% on Goal ($+$39.2\\pp). The advantage persists on Long (90.2\\% \\vs\\ 44.6\\%, $+$45.6\\pp), though Long horizon introduces a 3.8\\pp\\ gap from baseline. Attention-mean and attention-rollout show similar or slightly better performance on Spatial and Long suites. At more aggressive compression ($k\\!=\\!0.3$), attention-mean maintains 96.0\\% on Object and 90.4\\% on Spatial, while L2-norm collapses to 22.8\\% and 58.8\\% respectively.\n368\t\n369\tThis demonstrates that the pruning failure of entangling connectors under L2-norm is a \\emph{metric failure}, not a fundamental information limit. Attention weights directly encode the cross-token dependencies created by the BART encoder, enabling correct identification of critical tokens.\n370\t\n371\t\n372\t% ------------------------------------------------------------\n373\t\\subsection{VTR: Near-Lossless Compression via Learned Re-Encoding}\n374\t\\label{sec:vtr}\n375\t% ------------------------------------------------------------\n376\t\n377\tFor SmolVLA, where all deletion-based methods fail (including attention-based, which requires token-level identifiability), we propose VTR. Table~\\ref{tab:vtr_msweep} shows VTR performance across $M$ values on SmolVLA.\n378\t\n379\t\\begin{table}[t]\n380\t\\centering\n381\t\\caption{SmolVLA VTR M-sweep (3-seed). The M-curve is flat: all $M$ values achieve near-baseline SR (59.2\\%), including 87.5\\% compression ($M\\!=\\!16$). Hard pruning at 50\\% achieves 0\\%.}\n382\t\\label{tab:vtr_msweep}\n383\t\\small\n384\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccl}\n385\t\\toprule\n386\t$M$ & Compression & SR (\\%, mean$\\pm$std) & $\\Delta$ vs baseline (\\pp) & $p$ vs $M\\!=\\!128$ \\\\\n387\t\\midrule\n388\t16 & 87.5\\% & 56.9$\\pm$1.3 & $-$2.3 & $>$0.25 \\\\\n389\t32 & 75\\% & 60.1$\\pm$1.3 & $+$0.9 & $>$0.25 \\\\\n390\t64 & 50\\% & 62.0$\\pm$2.3 & $+$2.8 & $>$0.25 \\\\\n391\t96 & 25\\% & 54.5$\\pm$3.2 & $-$4.7 & 0.034$^*$ \\\\\n392\t128 & 0\\% (passthrough) & 58.0$\\pm$3.5 & $-$1.2 & --- \\\\\n393\t\\midrule\n394\tMLP-adapter@32 & 75\\% & 63.4$\\pm$2.6 & $+$4.2 & --- \\\\\n395\tHard pruning $k\\!=\\!0.5$ & 50\\% & 0.0 & $-$59.2 & --- \\\\\n396\t\\bottomrule\n397\t\\end{tabular}\n398\t\\end{table}\n399\t\n400\tThe M-curve is remarkably flat: $M\\!=\\!16$ (87.5\\% compression, 56.9\\%) through $M\\!=\\!128$ (passthrough, 58.0\\%), all within $\\pm$3\\pp\\ of baseline 59.2\\%. VTR achieves near-lossless compression at \\emph{any} ratio for pixel-shuffle connectors. The recovery ratio at $M\\!=\\!128$ is 0.98, confirming VTR recovers to---rather than exceeds---baseline.\n401\t\n402\t\\textbf{Architecture independence}: An MLP adapter with matched parameter count ($\\sim$22M) at $M\\!=\\!32$ achieves 63.4\\%$\\pm$2.6\\%, with no significant difference from cross-attention VTR ($p\\!=\\!0.15$). Any learned re-encoding mechanism repairs pixel-shuffle entanglement.\n403\t\n404\t\\textbf{Cross-suite generalization} (Table~\\ref{tab:vtr_crosssuite}). VTR recovery generalizes across all four suites. Recovery ratios range from 0.93 (Goal) to 1.21 (Spatial), centered around 1.0, confirming VTR repairs architectural entanglement rather than task-specific information.\n405\t\n406\t\\begin{table}[t]\n407\t\\centering\n408\t\\caption{VTR $M\\!=\\!32$ cross-suite recovery on SmolVLA. VTR consistently recovers from the 0\\% hard-pruning cliff-edge to near-baseline on all suites, with recovery ratios near 1.0.}\n409\t\\label{tab:vtr_crosssuite}\n410\t\\small\n411\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n412\t\\toprule\n413\tSuite & Baseline SR (\\%) & Hard prune $k\\!=\\!0.5$ & VTR $M\\!=\\!32$ SR (\\%) & Recovery ratio \\\\\n414\t\\midrule\n415\tObject & 59.2 & 0.0 & 60.1 & 1.015 \\\\\n416\tSpatial & 43.8 & 0.6 & 53.2 & 1.21 \\\\\n417\tGoal & 60.8 & 0.0 & 56.4 & 0.93 \\\\\n418\tLong & 36.6 & 0.0 & 35.5 & 0.97 \\\\\n419\t\\bottomrule\n420\t\\end{tabular}\n421\t\\end{table}\n422\t\n423\t\n424\t% --- VTR Connector Dependency ---\n425\t\n426\t\\textbf{Connector-dependent ceiling} (Table~\\ref{tab:vtr_connector}). VTR's effectiveness is strictly connector-dependent. On OpenVLA (MLP projector), VTR at $M\\!=\\!128$ achieves 0\\% SR (severe overfitting, identical action output per step). VTC-only variants at $M\\!=\\!32$, $M\\!=\\!64$, and $M\\!=\\!96$ all achieve 0\\% SR on OpenVLA. Training loss converges, but learned representations do not transfer to closed-loop control.\n427\t\n428\t\\begin{table}[t]\n429\t\\centering\n430\t\\caption{VTR connector-dependency summary. VTR rescues pixel-shuffle entanglement ($+$60.1\\pp) but fails on MLP projectors ($-$37.4\\pp\\ vs hard pruning). For MLP, simple deletion works; for pixel-shuffle, only re-encoding works.}\n431\t\\label{tab:vtr_connector}\n432\t\\small\n433\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n434\t\\toprule\n435\tConnector & Model & CV & Hard prune $k\\!=\\!0.5$ & VTR $M\\!=\\!32$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n436\t\\midrule\n437\tPixel-shuffle & SmolVLA & 0.314 & 0.0\\% & 60.1\\% & $+$60.1 \\\\\n438\tMLP (3-layer) & OpenVLA & 0.215 & 37.4\\% & 0.0\\% & $-$37.4 \\\\\n439\tLinear+BART & X-VLA & 0.151 & 42.6/70.6 & N/A & --- \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01STXSqAQ3t5rr6naL2y5ZvK","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"7b8c61b9-8000-4f5a-b8cc-5482efb13cee","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:20:51.108Z"}
