{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a808878398aa3fc09","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LUSxDsPyqr6gx8jzrpSmku","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":74550,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":136636},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"d666c413-d9b3-473c-a316-528a401d6e7c","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HN6V6YTqKpxS6P94HkxpGS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PNAoAnWzoNfNMqwSaYLzaS","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"importance_margin_vla_compress","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260525T050917","overall_score":"6 - Marginally Above Acceptance Threshold","report":"# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Focus\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents an empirical study of vision token pruning across six VLA architectures, organized along two axes: token coupling (determined by the vision connector) and task complexity. The authors show that connector architecture governs pruning robustness---token-independent connectors (linear, MLP) degrade gracefully while token-entangling connectors (pixel-shuffle, BART encoder) exhibit catastrophic or threshold-graded collapse. They propose the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms as a single-pass diagnostic, demonstrate a double dissociation between scoring methods (attention vs. L2 norm) and connector types, and introduce Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) to recover pixel-shuffle models from 0% to ~60% SR at 75% compression.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Breadth of architecture coverage.** Evaluating 6 VLAs across 3 connector families is substantially more comprehensive than any prior VLA pruning paper. The inclusion of models spanning 450M to 7B parameters and diverse connector designs (linear, MLP, BART encoder, pixel-shuffle) makes the findings broadly relevant.\n\n- **Strong experimental design for SmolVLA collapse.** The pixel-shuffle catastrophic failure claim is supported by exhaustive ablation: multiple checkpoints (44.8% and 59.2% baselines), multiple scoring methods (ACIS, random, L2 norm), multiple mechanisms (zero-mask, true removal, ToMe merging), and all retention ratios. This is the most convincing result in the paper.\n\n- **Double dissociation is cleanly demonstrated.** The attention-vs-L2 scoring crossover between X-VLA and pi0.5 (Table 5 in the main text / scoring crossover table) is a genuine scientific finding. The data are compelling: attention achieves 99.2% on X-VLA Object at k=0.5 vs. L2's 42.6%, while on pi0.5 Object attention collapses to 37.8% vs. random's 68.2%. This is not a marginal effect.\n\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper forthrightly acknowledges simulation-only evaluation, confounders (backbone, encoder, scale all vary alongside connector), the small sample for CV-SR correlation (4 closed-loop models), single-seed for most conditions, and the VTR-only-on-SmolVLA limitation. This level of candor is appreciated and appropriate.\n\n- **VTR as mechanistic probe.** Using VTR not merely as a practical solution but as evidence that *deletion itself* (not redundancy) is the failure mechanism is a nice experimental design choice. The MLP adapter control (p=0.15 vs. cross-attention) strengthens the argument that re-encoding generically, not cross-attention specifically, drives the recovery.\n\n- **Actionable diagnostic.** The CV metric is cheap to compute (one forward pass) and partitions models into interpretable classes. While validated on only 4 closed-loop models, it provides a concrete practical tool that practitioners can immediately apply.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Causal claims are overclaimed relative to experimental design.** The six architectures differ in backbone (PaliGemma, Llama-2, Qwen2), vision encoder (SigLIP variants, DINOv2+SigLIP), parameter count (16x range), token count (50 to 768), and action head design---all simultaneously with connector type. The paper acknowledges confounders in the appendix but the main text frequently uses language suggesting the connector is *the* causal factor (e.g., \"the critical difference is not model scale but the connector architecture\" in the introduction). The pi0-OpenVLA comparison is the closest controlled pair, yet it still differs in backbone and encoder fusion. A matched-backbone ablation (same LLM, same encoder, swapping only the connector) would be needed for causal claims. As written, the correct framing is \"connector architecture is *associated with* pruning robustness\" rather than \"determines\" it.\n\n- **CV diagnostic validated on only 4 closed-loop models (effectively 3 distinct CV values for the interesting region).** With SmolVLA at 0% regardless, the CV-SR relationship is really established across pi0.5 (0.055), X-VLA (0.151), and OpenVLA (0.215). Three data points cannot establish a \"scaling law\" or even a robust correlation. The Spearman rho=1.0 mentioned in the dream report is trivially achievable with 3 points. The paper should be more cautious about the generalizability of CV as a diagnostic.\n\n- **Incomplete coverage of the experimental matrix.** Table 2 (main results) has significant gaps marked \"--\": X-VLA Spatial and Long at k=0.7, OpenVLA has only Object suite data. The cross-suite analysis that is central to the \"task complexity axis\" claim is largely driven by X-VLA and pi0.5. OpenVLA's response to task complexity is entirely unknown from the presented data. The paper's 2-axis framework requires demonstrating the interaction for all (or most) connector types across task complexity, not just for the two models that happened to have complete data.\n\n- **Statistical rigor is inconsistent.** Multi-seed evaluation (3 seeds) is reported for VTR and X-VLA cliff localization, but most main results use a single seed. The main table explicitly states \"All results use seed 42.\" For a paper whose central claim is about *qualitative differences* between architectures, single-seed results are acceptable for the clear-cut cases (SmolVLA 0% across everything) but problematic for the nuanced claims about method gaps and non-monotonicity. The pi0.5 random disruption peak at k=0.85 is confirmed with 2 seeds, which helps, but the k=0.925 data point (27.6% on Object) appears to be single-seed.\n\n- **VTR universality claim is undermined by the OpenVLA failure.** VTR on OpenVLA produces 0% SR despite converged training loss, and the LoRA baseline also produces 0%. This suggests the OpenVLA fine-tuning pipeline itself is broken rather than VTR being architecture-dependent. The paper frames this as \"VTR fails on MLP projectors,\" but an alternative explanation is that the authors could not successfully fine-tune OpenVLA with their compute setup (batch size 4 vs. the original's much larger batch). The paper should more carefully distinguish \"VTR is architecture-dependent\" from \"our training setup was insufficient for OpenVLA.\"\n\n- **No latency or throughput measurements for the pruning methods on larger models.** The paper motivates token pruning as solving a \"computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control\" but provides latency numbers only for SmolVLA VTR (154ms vs. 164ms, 6% improvement). For the models where pruning actually works (pi0.5 at 4B, OpenVLA at 7B), no inference speed measurements are reported. The practical significance of the findings for deployment is unclear.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the OpenVLA VTR failure:** The LoRA baseline for OpenVLA also achieves 0% SR. Does this mean your fine-tuning setup (batch size 4, single GPU) is fundamentally unable to produce a working OpenVLA policy? If so, the VTR failure on OpenVLA cannot be attributed to the MLP connector---it is a training infrastructure limitation. Can you clarify whether you successfully reproduced *any* non-zero OpenVLA fine-tuning result with your setup?\n\n2. **On the CV diagnostic's predictive power for new architectures:** You validate CV on 4 closed-loop models. What would you predict for a Perceiver Resampler connector (e.g., RoboFlamingo, which appears in the dream report but was dropped from the paper)? The dream report mentions this was planned. Can you provide the CV value and explain why it was excluded?\n\n3. **On the non-monotonic recovery at k<=0.7 for pi0.5 L2 pruning:** The V-shaped curve (SR drops from 98% to 33% at k=0.5, then recovers to 53% at k=0.3) is striking and unexplained. You note it \"remains unexplained\" and hypothesize a \"qualitatively different computation regime.\" Given that this is a core finding about L2 norm behavior on low-CV models, have you investigated whether this is an artifact of position re-indexing? Specifically, does the V-shape disappear if you use zero-masking instead of physical removal?\n\n4. **On attention pruning generalization:** Attention-based pruning is \"fully evaluated on X-VLA\" and partially on pi0.5. For the practical recommendation to be useful, do you have any attention pruning data for OpenVLA? The ROADMAP suggests OpenVLA attention results exist for some suites (Object: 61.4%, Goal: 73.2%, Spatial: 80.4%). Why are these not in the paper?\n\n5. **On the \"20x amplification\" claim:** You state \"X-VLA loses just 1.4pp on Object but 27.8pp on Goal, a 20x amplification.\" This is computed as 27.8/1.4 = 19.9x. But this ratio is extremely sensitive to the denominator: if Object lost 2.0pp instead of 1.4pp, the \"amplification\" would be 14x. Given that 1.4pp is within typical evaluation noise for 500 episodes, how robust is this specific quantitative claim?\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments / Ablations\n\n- **OpenVLA cross-suite evaluation.** The paper's 2-axis framework requires showing the task complexity interaction for the MLP connector family, not just linear and BART. OpenVLA results on Spatial, Goal, and Long are essential for the framework's completeness. The ROADMAP shows these experiments were in progress---if available, they should be included.\n\n- **Zero-masking vs. physical removal ablation on pi0.5.** The non-monotonic behavior and disruption peaks may be artifacts of position re-indexing (which the paper acknowledges contributes via RoPE). A direct comparison would clarify how much of the observed behavior is pruning-intrinsic vs. position-encoding-induced.\n\n- **Attention pruning on pi0.5 at retention ratios other than k=0.5.** The current data show attention is harmful on Object at k=0.5 but neutral on other suites. Does this pattern hold at k=0.9 where L2 excels? The double dissociation would be more convincing with matched retention ratios.\n\n- **Real-time inference benchmarks** for pi0.5 and X-VLA under pruning. Even simulation-based wall-clock measurements of action prediction latency vs. retention ratio would strengthen the practical motivation.\n\n- **At least one additional model per connector family** for CV validation. Even an offline-only model with closed-loop proxy metrics would help. The OFT model (CV=0.219, MLP) has no SR evaluation due to GPU constraints---is there any way to obtain even a partial evaluation?\n\n## 6. Minor Issues\n\n- Table 2 caption says \"Best result per row in bold\" but bold appears to be applied to baselines, which are trivially always best (unpruned). Clarify that bold marks the best *pruned* result or the baseline.\n\n- The paper mentions \"RoboFlamingo\" and \"Perceiver Resampler\" in the dream report's experimental setup (Section 3) but these are absent from the actual submitted paper with no explanation of the discrepancy. If this connector family was dropped, a brief mention of why would be informative.\n\n- The notation for retention ratio k and compression ratio could be more consistent. In the VTR section, \"75% compression\" means M=32 out of 128 tokens (75% *removed*), while k=0.5 means 50% *retained*. These conventions are opposite and could confuse readers.\n\n- Appendix Table 12 (OpenVLA full degradation) reports seed 7 results while the main table uses seed 42. The inconsistent seed choices across models make cross-model comparisons harder to interpret.\n\n- The hypergeometric cliff model (Appendix) is fit to 3 data points with 2 free parameters (C and T). This is almost certainly overfit and the RMSE of 0.018 is meaningless with 1 degree of freedom. It should be presented as a qualitative illustration, not a quantitative model.\n\n- Minor: \"scoring validity is architecture-dependent\" appears in both the abstract and related work in nearly identical phrasing.\n\n## 7. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Soundness | 3 (Good) |\n| Presentation | 3 (Good) |\n| Contribution | 3 (Good) |\n| Overall Score | 6 (Marginally above acceptance threshold) |\n| Confidence | 4 (High confidence; reviewed carefully, familiar with the area) |\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles an important practical question (when does token pruning work for VLAs?) with commendable breadth. The SmolVLA collapse finding and the double dissociation between scoring methods are genuine contributions. However, the incomplete experimental matrix (OpenVLA limited to one suite, attention pruning limited to one architecture in full), the causal overclaiming from observational data with many confounders, and the thin statistical basis for the CV diagnostic (3-4 models) prevent a higher score. The work is above the bar for a solid empirical contribution at CoRL but would benefit from closing the experimental gaps before final submission. The simulation-only limitation is acceptable for a systematic study paper but limits the significance for a robotics venue.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":73412,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":725,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LUSxDsPyqr6gx8jzrpSmku","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"6c69cdea-a26d-44a3-a527-8f62b2c3e218"}
