{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01STXSqAQ3t5rr6naL2y5ZvK","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The review has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (marginally below acceptance)**\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- First cross-architecture VLA token pruning study (6 models, 4 connector families, 4 task suites) -- genuinely the broadest evaluation in this space\n- The scoring-connector double dissociation (attention effective on entangling connectors, L2-norm effective on independent connectors) is the most genuinely novel finding\n- Pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff (0% at 5% removal) is thoroughly validated and important for the community\n- Honest reporting of framework failures and limitations\n\n**Key weaknesses identified:**\n- CV diagnostic validated on only 3 closed-loop models -- Spearman rho=1.0 with n=3 is trivially achievable\n- Causal claims (\"connector determines pruning robustness\") exceed the correlational evidence, since models differ in backbone, encoder, scale, and token count simultaneously\n- LIBERO-only simulation evaluation limits CoRL significance; no real-robot or multi-simulator validation\n- Related work section is too thin; insufficient engagement with concurrent works (VLA-Pruner, Don't Run with Scissors, token merging literature)\n- The \"2-axis framework\" (connector coupling x task complexity) contributes less novelty than presented -- the individual axes are expected observations\n- Sparse data matrix (many \"--\" entries in Table 1) undermines the \"comprehensive study\" claim\n\n**Scores:** Soundness 3/4, Presentation 3/4, Contribution 2/4, Overall 5/10, Confidence 4/5.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T050917/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab55b1c22199f5b60 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab55b1c22199f5b60' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 127913\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 183270</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"e78c0bfb-23b1-48cb-bceb-38da50f2f37f","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:22:58.280Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\nCoRL is the Conference on Robot Learning. Acceptance rate ~25-33%. Review dimensions:\n- **Originality**: Are the problems/methods new? Novel combination of known techniques?\n- **Quality**: Technically sound? Claims well-supported?\n- **Clarity**: Well written, well organized?\n- **Significance**: Important results? Substantial contribution?\n- **Relevance**: Addresses robot learning?\n- **Limitations**: Honestly reported?\n\nScoring scale (1-10): 1=strong reject, 3=clear reject, 5=marginally below, 6=marginally above, 7=good paper accept, 8=strong accept, 10=top 5%.\n\n## Your Role\nFocus on: novelty assessment, related work coverage, positioning against concurrent work, contribution significance for the CoRL community, narrative coherence, whether findings are surprising vs expected.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures\"\n\nThis is an empirical study paper (not a method paper). It evaluates token pruning across 5 VLA models, 3 connector families, 4 LIBERO task suites, and 3 importance metrics.\n\n**Core Claims**:\n1. Pixel-shuffle connectors create irreversible entanglement (SmolVLA: 0% SR at k≤0.95 across all suites/methods)\n2. Importance metric selection > connector architecture (X-VLA: attention pruning 99.2% vs L2norm 42.6% at k=0.5)\n3. VTR recovers pixel-shuffle models (0%→59%≈baseline) but fails on MLP models (0% across all configs)\n\n**Related Work mentioned in paper**:\n- VLA models: π0 [2], π0.5, SmolVLA [3], X-VLA [4], OpenVLA [1]\n- Token pruning: FastV [6], VLA-IAP [8], ToMe, EfficientVLA [7], LightVLA [9], Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, See What Matters [10]\n- LIBERO benchmark [5]\n\n**Paper Structure**:\n- Abstract → Introduction (3 contributions) → Background/Related Work (brief, ~1 page) → Experimental Setup → Results (12 subsections, Tables 1-11) → Discussion (4 subsections) → Conclusion\n- 7 pages total + references\n- 11 tables, no figures mentioned in text\n\n**Key narrative**: The paper was originally conceived as discovering a \"principled 2-axis framework\" for predicting VLA pruning behavior, but the cross-suite data broke this framework (only 62% prediction accuracy). The paper was reframed as a \"comprehensive empirical study\" with three surviving findings.\n\n**Experiment Design Reference** shows:\n- The paper started with a 2-axis framework (Axis 1: token coupling via connector type, Axis 2: CV as universal predictor) but this framework failed cross-suite validation\n- Table 11 (Claim Survival) explicitly shows C1 (2-axis framework) and C5 (CV scaling law) \"fail\" — the paper honestly reports these failures\n- The paper positions itself as \"empirical study with surprising findings\" rather than a theory paper\n- The authors estimate ~45% acceptance probability at CoRL\n\n**What concurrent works do**:\n- EfficientVLA [7]: training-free pruning for VLAs (2025)\n- VLA-IAP [8]: inference-aware pruning (2025)\n- LightVLA [9]: light token pruning, ICRA 2026\n- See What Matters [10]: token importance estimation, ICML 2026\n- These works focus on single architectures; none do cross-architecture comparison\n\n**Missing from related work** (things that should be discussed):\n- Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA mentioned by name only\n- No discussion of token merging (ToMe) vs token pruning differences\n- No comparison with knowledge distillation approaches for VLA compression\n- Limited discussion of how findings relate to VLM (not VLA) pruning literature (FastV, etc.)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences, your own words)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted list, 4-6 items, focus on novelty/significance/positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, 4-6 items, focus on novelty gaps, related work, positioning issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Novelty Assessment**: Is this sufficiently novel for CoRL? Is it an incremental benchmark paper or does it provide genuine new insights?\n6. **Minor Issues**\n7. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-4)\n   - Presentation (1-4)\n   - Contribution (1-4)\n   - Overall Score (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider whether the robotics/robot learning community would benefit from this work. Assess whether the \"negative results\" (framework failure) add value or suggest the work is incomplete.\n","agentId":"ab55b1c22199f5b60","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The review has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 5/10 (marginally below acceptance)**\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- First cross-architecture VLA token pruning study (6 models, 4 connector families, 4 task suites) -- genuinely the broadest evaluation in this space\n- The scoring-connector double dissociation (attention effective on entangling connectors, L2-norm effective on independent connectors) is the most genuinely novel finding\n- Pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff (0% at 5% removal) is thoroughly validated and important for the community\n- Honest reporting of framework failures and limitations\n\n**Key weaknesses identified:**\n- CV diagnostic validated on only 3 closed-loop models -- Spearman rho=1.0 with n=3 is trivially achievable\n- Causal claims (\"connector determines pruning robustness\") exceed the correlational evidence, since models differ in backbone, encoder, scale, and token count simultaneously\n- LIBERO-only simulation evaluation limits CoRL significance; no real-robot or multi-simulator validation\n- Related work section is too thin; insufficient engagement with concurrent works (VLA-Pruner, Don't Run with Scissors, token merging literature)\n- The \"2-axis framework\" (connector coupling x task complexity) contributes less novelty than presented -- the individual axes are expected observations\n- Sparse data matrix (many \"--\" entries in Table 1) undermines the \"comprehensive study\" claim\n\n**Scores:** Soundness 3/4, Presentation 3/4, Contribution 2/4, Overall 5/10, Confidence 4/5.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T050917/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":183270,"totalTokens":127913,"totalToolUseCount":27,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4325,"cache_read_input_tokens":123142,"output_tokens":445,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4325},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":445,"cache_read_input_tokens":123142,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4325,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
