{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7ByEAW4hzD2piGW2ioXFn","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n# Review: A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures\n\n**Reviewer 1 — Methods & Experiments Focus**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of visual token pruning strategies across five Vision-Language-Action (VLA) architectures on the LIBERO benchmark suite. The authors evaluate three pruning methods (attention-based, L2-norm-based, and Vision Token Re-merging) across four LIBERO task suites, revealing key findings about architectural interactions: pixel-shuffle connectors completely block pruning, importance metric selection matters more than connector type, and VTR effectiveness is architecture-dependent. A predictive framework is proposed to anticipate pruning compatibility from architectural features, achieving approximately 62% accuracy.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Breadth of the experimental matrix.** A 5-model × 4-suite × 3-method evaluation is a substantial undertaking. The combinatorial coverage (60 primary configurations before ablations) provides a landscape view that no prior single-architecture pruning paper offers, making this a genuinely useful reference for practitioners choosing a compression strategy for a specific VLA.\n\n- **Identification of the pixel-shuffle entanglement failure mode.** The finding that SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector yields 0% success under naive token pruning is a concrete, actionable insight. This is not obvious a priori — pixel-shuffle is popular in vision backbones — and the paper provides a clear mechanistic explanation (spatial entanglement of sub-pixel features across token positions makes individual token removal destructive).\n\n- **Importance metric vs. connector architecture finding.** Demonstrating that the choice of importance metric (attention vs. L2-norm) can swing success rates from 42.6% to 99.2% on X-VLA is a strong result. This disentangles two axes of variation that prior work confounds and gives clear guidance: attention-based importance is broadly more reliable than L2-norm across architectures.\n\n- **Thorough per-suite breakdown across LIBERO.** Reporting results separately on LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Goal, and LIBERO-Long (rather than only an aggregate) reveals meaningful variation in task difficulty under pruning. The Long-horizon suite serves as a stress test that exposes fragility invisible in simpler suites.\n\n- **Reproducibility effort.** The paper describes the evaluation protocol, pruning implementation details, and hyperparameters with enough specificity that replication appears feasible. The use of a standardized benchmark (LIBERO) rather than custom environments strengthens this.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Predictive framework is underdeveloped (62% accuracy).** The proposed framework for predicting pruning compatibility from architectural features achieves only ~62% accuracy, which is barely above a majority-class baseline depending on the label distribution. The paper presents this as a contribution, but at this accuracy level it is more of a preliminary observation than a reliable tool. The features used, the classification methodology, and the failure cases are insufficiently analyzed. What architectural features does the framework use? How was it validated (cross-validation, held-out architecture)? With only 5 architectures, overfitting or memorization is a serious concern, and leave-one-out would leave very few training points.\n\n- **No latency, throughput, or memory measurements.** For a paper motivated by computational efficiency of VLA deployment, the absence of wall-clock inference time, tokens-per-second, GPU memory footprint, or FLOPs measurements is a significant gap. Success rate retention tells us whether pruning preserves task performance, but without efficiency metrics we cannot assess the actual practical benefit. A method that retains 99% success but only saves 5% compute is uninteresting; one that retains 90% success while halving latency may be very interesting. The paper never quantifies this tradeoff.\n\n- **Single pruning ratio regime.** The experiments appear to evaluate a fixed or narrow range of pruning ratios. A systematic study should include Pareto curves of success rate vs. pruning ratio (e.g., 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%) for each method-architecture pair. Without this, we cannot determine whether the failures reported are fundamental incompatibilities or whether they arise only at aggressive pruning levels. The difference between \"this method fails at 50% pruning\" and \"this method fails at any pruning\" is critical for practitioners.\n\n- **LIBERO-only evaluation limits generalizability claims.** While LIBERO is a reasonable benchmark, it is a simulated tabletop manipulation suite. The paper's title and framing suggest general conclusions about VLA token pruning, but all evidence comes from one simulator with one robot morphology and one observation modality setup. Real-world deployment, different embodiments (mobile manipulation, navigation), or different visual complexity (outdoor, cluttered) could yield different architecture-pruning interactions. The paper should either scope its claims more carefully or include at least one additional benchmark domain.\n\n- **Lack of fine-tuning or recovery training.** All pruning is applied post-hoc without any adaptation. This is a valid evaluation axis, but presenting it as the only axis overstates the severity of observed failures. Many token pruning methods in LLM/VLM literature include a brief fine-tuning phase to recover accuracy. Without testing whether a small amount of fine-tuning (even a few hundred steps) can recover the 0% cases (SmolVLA) or the low-performing configurations, the paper's conclusions about architectural incompatibility are potentially too strong.\n\n- **Statistical reporting is thin.** With 11 tables but limited mention of confidence intervals, standard deviations across seeds, or significance tests, it is difficult to assess whether the reported differences (e.g., 99.2% vs. 97.8%) are meaningful or within noise. How many evaluation episodes per configuration? How many random seeds? LIBERO success rates can be noisy, especially on Long-horizon tasks.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Pruning ratio sensitivity:** What pruning ratio(s) were used in the main experiments? Have you evaluated how success rates degrade as a function of pruning ratio for each architecture-method pair? Even a coarse sweep (25%, 50%, 75%) would substantially strengthen the conclusions.\n\n2. **Predictive framework details:** Can you provide the full specification of the predictive framework — what input features are used, what classifier, and what validation procedure? With N=5 architectures, how do you guard against memorization? What does \"62% accuracy\" mean concretely — accuracy over what set of predictions?\n\n3. **Recovery fine-tuning:** Have you attempted even lightweight fine-tuning (e.g., 500-1000 gradient steps on LIBERO demonstrations) after pruning to see if the catastrophic failures (SmolVLA 0%, OpenVLA VTR failure) can be partially recovered? This would help distinguish \"fundamentally incompatible\" from \"requires adaptation.\"\n\n4. **Efficiency measurements:** Can you report inference latency or throughput for pruned vs. unpruned models? This would contextualize which pruning configurations are actually worth pursuing from a deployment standpoint.\n\n5. **VTR architecture dependence:** You report that VTR recovers SmolVLA from 0% to 59% but fails on OpenVLA. Can you provide a deeper mechanistic analysis of why? Is it related to the vision encoder, the connector, or the LLM backbone? Ablating these components would strengthen the analysis beyond an empirical observation.\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments / Ablations\n\n- **Pruning ratio Pareto curves** for all architecture-method combinations (most critical missing experiment).\n- **Inference efficiency benchmarks** (latency, memory, FLOPs) with and without pruning.\n- **Recovery fine-tuning experiments** to separate fundamental incompatibility from lack of adaptation.\n- **Ablation of the predictive framework features** — which architectural properties are most predictive?\n- **At least one additional evaluation domain** beyond LIBERO (e.g., MetaWorld, RLBench, or a real-robot setting) to test generalization of the findings.\n- **Layer-wise pruning analysis** — are certain layers' tokens more important than others? Does pruning only early/late layers change the architecture-dependence story?\n- **Combination strategies** — what happens when combining two pruning methods (e.g., attention-based selection + VTR for re-merging)?\n\n## 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper has 11 tables in 7 pages, which creates a very dense reading experience. Several tables could be consolidated or moved to an appendix, with the main text featuring only the most critical comparisons and perhaps a summary figure.\n- The term \"importance margin\" appears in related project context but its precise definition in this paper's framework could be clarified earlier.\n- Some table captions are terse and would benefit from noting the pruning ratio and number of evaluation episodes used.\n- The related work section could better contextualize token pruning in the broader VLM efficiency literature (e.g., FastV, ToMe for ViTs, LLM KV-cache pruning) to clarify what is novel about the VLA-specific study.\n- Figure quality and readability should be checked — with this many configurations, bar charts or heatmaps might communicate results more effectively than tables alone.\n\n## 7. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 2 (some concerns) |\n| **Presentation** | 3 (good) |\n| **Contribution** | 3 (good) |\n| **Overall Score** | 6 (marginally above acceptance threshold) |\n| **Confidence** | 4 (confident — familiar with VLA architectures and token pruning literature) |\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a timely and practically relevant question — which pruning strategies work for which VLA architectures — and the experimental breadth is commendable. The pixel-shuffle finding and importance-metric-vs-connector finding are genuinely useful. However, the lack of efficiency measurements undermines the core motivation, the single pruning ratio limits the depth of conclusions, the predictive framework is too preliminary to be a real contribution at 62% accuracy, and the absence of any recovery fine-tuning experiments means the paper may overstate architectural incompatibilities. With the missing experiments addressed (particularly pruning ratio sweeps and latency measurements), this could become a solid empirical contribution. In its current form, it is borderline — useful observations but incomplete as a systematic study."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abf40baead36826e3 (use SendMessage with to: 'abf40baead36826e3' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16986\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 65385</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"623ee561-0a54-4f3b-84a1-378bfdd98373","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:24:17.891Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You previously reviewed a CoRL 2026 paper titled \"A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures\" as Reviewer 1 (methods + experiments focus).\n\nPlease provide your COMPLETE review in the following format. Do not abbreviate — give the full text of every section:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, 4-6 items with explanations)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, 4-6 items with detailed explanations)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 numbered questions)\n5. **Missing Experiments / Ablations**\n6. **Minor Issues**\n7. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-4)\n   - Presentation (1-4)\n   - Contribution (1-4)\n   - Overall Score (1-10)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nHere is the paper information for context (you already reviewed it):\n\nTitle: \"A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures\"\n- 5 VLA models × 4 LIBERO suites × 3 pruning methods\n- Key findings: pixel-shuffle entanglement (SmolVLA 0%), importance metric > connector architecture (X-VLA attention 99.2% vs L2norm 42.6%), VTR architecture-specific (SmolVLA recovery 0%→59%, OpenVLA fail)\n- Framework prediction accuracy ~62%\n- 11 tables, 7 pages\n\nYour overall score was 6 (marginally above). Please reproduce the FULL detailed review.\n","agentId":"abf40baead36826e3","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n# Review: A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures\n\n**Reviewer 1 — Methods & Experiments Focus**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of visual token pruning strategies across five Vision-Language-Action (VLA) architectures on the LIBERO benchmark suite. The authors evaluate three pruning methods (attention-based, L2-norm-based, and Vision Token Re-merging) across four LIBERO task suites, revealing key findings about architectural interactions: pixel-shuffle connectors completely block pruning, importance metric selection matters more than connector type, and VTR effectiveness is architecture-dependent. A predictive framework is proposed to anticipate pruning compatibility from architectural features, achieving approximately 62% accuracy.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Breadth of the experimental matrix.** A 5-model × 4-suite × 3-method evaluation is a substantial undertaking. The combinatorial coverage (60 primary configurations before ablations) provides a landscape view that no prior single-architecture pruning paper offers, making this a genuinely useful reference for practitioners choosing a compression strategy for a specific VLA.\n\n- **Identification of the pixel-shuffle entanglement failure mode.** The finding that SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector yields 0% success under naive token pruning is a concrete, actionable insight. This is not obvious a priori — pixel-shuffle is popular in vision backbones — and the paper provides a clear mechanistic explanation (spatial entanglement of sub-pixel features across token positions makes individual token removal destructive).\n\n- **Importance metric vs. connector architecture finding.** Demonstrating that the choice of importance metric (attention vs. L2-norm) can swing success rates from 42.6% to 99.2% on X-VLA is a strong result. This disentangles two axes of variation that prior work confounds and gives clear guidance: attention-based importance is broadly more reliable than L2-norm across architectures.\n\n- **Thorough per-suite breakdown across LIBERO.** Reporting results separately on LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Goal, and LIBERO-Long (rather than only an aggregate) reveals meaningful variation in task difficulty under pruning. The Long-horizon suite serves as a stress test that exposes fragility invisible in simpler suites.\n\n- **Reproducibility effort.** The paper describes the evaluation protocol, pruning implementation details, and hyperparameters with enough specificity that replication appears feasible. The use of a standardized benchmark (LIBERO) rather than custom environments strengthens this.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Predictive framework is underdeveloped (62% accuracy).** The proposed framework for predicting pruning compatibility from architectural features achieves only ~62% accuracy, which is barely above a majority-class baseline depending on the label distribution. The paper presents this as a contribution, but at this accuracy level it is more of a preliminary observation than a reliable tool. The features used, the classification methodology, and the failure cases are insufficiently analyzed. What architectural features does the framework use? How was it validated (cross-validation, held-out architecture)? With only 5 architectures, overfitting or memorization is a serious concern, and leave-one-out would leave very few training points.\n\n- **No latency, throughput, or memory measurements.** For a paper motivated by computational efficiency of VLA deployment, the absence of wall-clock inference time, tokens-per-second, GPU memory footprint, or FLOPs measurements is a significant gap. Success rate retention tells us whether pruning preserves task performance, but without efficiency metrics we cannot assess the actual practical benefit. A method that retains 99% success but only saves 5% compute is uninteresting; one that retains 90% success while halving latency may be very interesting. The paper never quantifies this tradeoff.\n\n- **Single pruning ratio regime.** The experiments appear to evaluate a fixed or narrow range of pruning ratios. A systematic study should include Pareto curves of success rate vs. pruning ratio (e.g., 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%) for each method-architecture pair. Without this, we cannot determine whether the failures reported are fundamental incompatibilities or whether they arise only at aggressive pruning levels. The difference between \"this method fails at 50% pruning\" and \"this method fails at any pruning\" is critical for practitioners.\n\n- **LIBERO-only evaluation limits generalizability claims.** While LIBERO is a reasonable benchmark, it is a simulated tabletop manipulation suite. The paper's title and framing suggest general conclusions about VLA token pruning, but all evidence comes from one simulator with one robot morphology and one observation modality setup. Real-world deployment, different embodiments (mobile manipulation, navigation), or different visual complexity (outdoor, cluttered) could yield different architecture-pruning interactions. The paper should either scope its claims more carefully or include at least one additional benchmark domain.\n\n- **Lack of fine-tuning or recovery training.** All pruning is applied post-hoc without any adaptation. This is a valid evaluation axis, but presenting it as the only axis overstates the severity of observed failures. Many token pruning methods in LLM/VLM literature include a brief fine-tuning phase to recover accuracy. Without testing whether a small amount of fine-tuning (even a few hundred steps) can recover the 0% cases (SmolVLA) or the low-performing configurations, the paper's conclusions about architectural incompatibility are potentially too strong.\n\n- **Statistical reporting is thin.** With 11 tables but limited mention of confidence intervals, standard deviations across seeds, or significance tests, it is difficult to assess whether the reported differences (e.g., 99.2% vs. 97.8%) are meaningful or within noise. How many evaluation episodes per configuration? How many random seeds? LIBERO success rates can be noisy, especially on Long-horizon tasks.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Pruning ratio sensitivity:** What pruning ratio(s) were used in the main experiments? Have you evaluated how success rates degrade as a function of pruning ratio for each architecture-method pair? Even a coarse sweep (25%, 50%, 75%) would substantially strengthen the conclusions.\n\n2. **Predictive framework details:** Can you provide the full specification of the predictive framework — what input features are used, what classifier, and what validation procedure? With N=5 architectures, how do you guard against memorization? What does \"62% accuracy\" mean concretely — accuracy over what set of predictions?\n\n3. **Recovery fine-tuning:** Have you attempted even lightweight fine-tuning (e.g., 500-1000 gradient steps on LIBERO demonstrations) after pruning to see if the catastrophic failures (SmolVLA 0%, OpenVLA VTR failure) can be partially recovered? This would help distinguish \"fundamentally incompatible\" from \"requires adaptation.\"\n\n4. **Efficiency measurements:** Can you report inference latency or throughput for pruned vs. unpruned models? This would contextualize which pruning configurations are actually worth pursuing from a deployment standpoint.\n\n5. **VTR architecture dependence:** You report that VTR recovers SmolVLA from 0% to 59% but fails on OpenVLA. Can you provide a deeper mechanistic analysis of why? Is it related to the vision encoder, the connector, or the LLM backbone? Ablating these components would strengthen the analysis beyond an empirical observation.\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments / Ablations\n\n- **Pruning ratio Pareto curves** for all architecture-method combinations (most critical missing experiment).\n- **Inference efficiency benchmarks** (latency, memory, FLOPs) with and without pruning.\n- **Recovery fine-tuning experiments** to separate fundamental incompatibility from lack of adaptation.\n- **Ablation of the predictive framework features** — which architectural properties are most predictive?\n- **At least one additional evaluation domain** beyond LIBERO (e.g., MetaWorld, RLBench, or a real-robot setting) to test generalization of the findings.\n- **Layer-wise pruning analysis** — are certain layers' tokens more important than others? Does pruning only early/late layers change the architecture-dependence story?\n- **Combination strategies** — what happens when combining two pruning methods (e.g., attention-based selection + VTR for re-merging)?\n\n## 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper has 11 tables in 7 pages, which creates a very dense reading experience. Several tables could be consolidated or moved to an appendix, with the main text featuring only the most critical comparisons and perhaps a summary figure.\n- The term \"importance margin\" appears in related project context but its precise definition in this paper's framework could be clarified earlier.\n- Some table captions are terse and would benefit from noting the pruning ratio and number of evaluation episodes used.\n- The related work section could better contextualize token pruning in the broader VLM efficiency literature (e.g., FastV, ToMe for ViTs, LLM KV-cache pruning) to clarify what is novel about the VLA-specific study.\n- Figure quality and readability should be checked — with this many configurations, bar charts or heatmaps might communicate results more effectively than tables alone.\n\n## 7. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 2 (some concerns) |\n| **Presentation** | 3 (good) |\n| **Contribution** | 3 (good) |\n| **Overall Score** | 6 (marginally above acceptance threshold) |\n| **Confidence** | 4 (confident — familiar with VLA architectures and token pruning literature) |\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a timely and practically relevant question — which pruning strategies work for which VLA architectures — and the experimental breadth is commendable. The pixel-shuffle finding and importance-metric-vs-connector finding are genuinely useful. However, the lack of efficiency measurements undermines the core motivation, the single pruning ratio limits the depth of conclusions, the predictive framework is too preliminary to be a real contribution at 62% accuracy, and the absence of any recovery fine-tuning experiments means the paper may overstate architectural incompatibilities. With the missing experiments addressed (particularly pruning ratio sweeps and latency measurements), this could become a solid empirical contribution. In its current form, it is borderline — useful observations but incomplete as a systematic study."}],"totalDurationMs":65385,"totalTokens":16986,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6318,"cache_read_input_tokens":8254,"output_tokens":2412,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6318},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2412,"