{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XsnDBtmxJBu13DS2sSbFut","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260525T050917\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 5 - marginally below acceptance threshold\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-25T06:22:47.345274+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Review Report — Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n11\t\n12\t**Venue**: CoRL 2026\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Summary\n17\t\n18\tThis paper presents an empirical study of vision token pruning across six VLA architectures (450M--7B), three connector families, and four LIBERO task suites. The central finding is that pruning robustness is governed by two axes: (1) token coupling imposed by the vision connector (independent vs. entangling), and (2) task complexity, which amplifies degradation on entangling connectors. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms is proposed as a zero-cost diagnostic that predicts pruning tolerance and optimal strategy choice. For pixel-shuffle connectors where deletion fails entirely, a Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) recovers near-baseline performance. Additionally, the paper identifies a double dissociation between scoring methods and connector types: attention-based scoring rescues entangling connectors where L2-norm fails, and vice versa.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Strengths\n23\t\n24\t- **Breadth and systematicity of evaluation.** Six VLA models spanning four connector families, four LIBERO suites, multiple retention ratios, multiple seeds, and three pruning strategies (L2, random, attention) constitute a substantially more comprehensive evaluation than any concurrent VLA pruning work. The cross-architecture, cross-task design is the paper's primary differentiator from VLA-IAP, EfficientVLA, LightVLA, and others that validate on a single architecture.\n25\t\n26\t- **Genuine and actionable negative results.** The SmolVLA pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff (0% SR at 5% removal, verified on official checkpoint and across two seeds) is a striking finding that the community needs to know. The fact that this is confirmed across all four suites, both pruning methods, and multiple checkpoints makes it one of the most thoroughly validated negative results in this space. Similarly, the finding that VTR fails on MLP projectors (0% SR) is an honest and useful negative.\n27\t\n28\t- **Double dissociation between scoring method and connector type.** The finding that attention-based scoring is effective on X-VLA but harmful on pi0.5, while L2-norm shows the opposite pattern, is genuinely novel and non-obvious. This goes beyond the typical \"method A beats method B\" framing to reveal a structural interaction that has practical implications for practitioners.\n29\t\n30\t- **CV as diagnostic has practical appeal.** A single-forward-pass, zero-cost metric that provides even a coarse indicator of compressibility type is useful for practitioners. The monotonic scaling law at k=0.5 and k=0.7, while based on only three models, is clean and well-presented.\n31\t\n32\t- **Honest reporting of limitations and framework failures.** The paper openly acknowledges that the 2-axis framework was originally conceived as a stronger predictive theory but was broken by cross-suite data. The limitations section and confounder analysis (Appendix F) are unusually forthright for a CoRL submission. The acknowledgment that \"the six architectures differ in backbone, encoder, and scale beyond the connector, preventing causal isolation\" is commendable.\n33\t\n34\t- **Rich appendix.** The hypergeometric cliff model, token position analysis, and per-task breakdowns provide substantial supplementary evidence that strengthens the main claims.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 3. Weaknesses\n39\t\n40\t- **The CV diagnostic is validated on too few closed-loop models to support the generality claimed.** The monotonic CV-scaling law rests on exactly three data points (pi0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA) with SmolVLA excluded (both methods 0%). The paper claims CV \"forms a continuous spectrum predicting both pruning tolerance and the optimal pruning strategy,\" but a perfect Spearman correlation with n=3 is trivially achievable. The paper sometimes hedges this (\"we expect the trend to generalize but acknowledge the limited sample\") but the abstract and introduction present it with stronger confidence than warranted. The addition of OFT (offline only, no closed-loop SR) and pi0 (offline only) pads the model count without adding closed-loop validation. RoboFlamingo is mentioned in the dream report but barely appears in the actual paper draft.\n41\t\n42\t- **Positioning against concurrent work is insufficient.** The related work section is notably thin (~0.5 pages). Several concurrent works are mentioned by name only (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA) without discussing their methods, findings, or how this paper's results relate. More critically:\n43\t  - VLA-Pruner (Liu et al., 2025) explicitly separates semantic vs. action-level token importance -- directly relevant to the double dissociation finding, yet receives only a single clause.\n44\t  - \"Don't Run with Scissors\" (Jabbour et al., 2025) analyzes VLM token pruning with a focus on when pruning fails -- the closest methodological analog in the VLM space -- yet gets one sentence distinguishing domain (VQA vs. robotics) without engaging with its analytical framework.\n45\t  - No discussion of how the connector-coupling axis relates to the broader VLM literature on Q-Former vs. linear projector compression (BLIP-2, Flamingo Perceiver). These architectures implement the same independent-vs-entangling spectrum in the VLM domain.\n46\t  - Token merging (ToMe) is tested as one SmolVLA ablation condition but never discussed as a conceptual alternative to pruning. Merging preserves information that pruning destroys -- a fundamental distinction relevant to the \"deletion is the failure mechanism\" claim.\n47\t\n48\t- **Causal claims exceed the experimental design.** The paper repeatedly states that \"the vision connector determines pruning robustness\" and \"connector architecture is the most consistent structural factor,\" but the six models differ in backbone (PaliGemma, Llama-2, Qwen2), encoder (SigLIP, DINOv2+SigLIP), scale (450M--7B), token count (50--768), and training data. The pi0-vs-OpenVLA comparison is presented as a controlled comparison (\"same token count, same encoder lineage, only connector differs\") but in fact they differ in backbone and encoder fusion strategy. The paper acknowledges this in the appendix but the main text often reads as if connector is causally isolated. A matched-backbone ablation (e.g., swapping connectors on the same backbone) would be needed for causal claims; without it, the paper should consistently frame findings as correlational.\n49\t\n50\t- **LIBERO-only evaluation limits significance for CoRL.** All results are in simulation, on a single benchmark family. LIBERO's four suites provide task diversity but share the same robot, workspace, and visual domain. The paper's practical recommendations (\"measure CV before deploying token pruning\") are untested on real robots, different embodiments, or even a second simulator. The abandoned SimplerEnv attempt (documented in memory.md) and the failed MetaWorld results (SmolVLA baseline 26.4%, all pruned conditions collapsed) suggest that the findings may not transfer cleanly. For a CoRL paper, the absence of any real-robot or multi-simulator validation is a significant gap.\n51\t\n52\t- **The \"2-axis framework\" contributes less novelty than presented.** Axis 1 (connector architecture matters) is essentially the observation that different architectures behave differently under perturbation -- an expected finding. Axis 2 (harder tasks are more sensitive to degradation) is similarly unsurprising: multi-step tasks compound errors, so any per-step degradation amplifies. The interaction (entangling connectors amplify more on hard tasks) adds value but is not deeply mechanistic -- it follows from the fact that entangling connectors create larger per-step disruptions. The paper would benefit from more rigorous mechanistic analysis (e.g., probing what information is lost at each connector type, analyzing attention pattern changes after pruning) rather than framing correlational observations as a \"framework.\"\n53\t\n54\t- **Missing data in key tables.** Table 1 (main results) has numerous \"--\" entries, particularly for OpenVLA (only Object suite data for most conditions). The paper claims a \"5 models x 4 suites\" study but the actual data matrix is quite sparse. OpenVLA cross-suite data beyond Object is largely absent from the main text. This undermines the comprehensiveness claim.\n55\t\n56\t---\n57\t\n58\t## 4. Questions for Authors\n59\t\n60\t1. **On CV generality**: The CV scaling law has n=3 closed-loop data points. Have you computed CV for any VLA models outside your study (e.g., CogACT, RT-2, Octo) to test whether CV values fall in predicted ranges? Even without closed-loop evaluation, showing that CV correctly predicts the connector family would strengthen the diagnostic.\n61\t\n62\t2. **On token merging vs. pruning**: You test ToMe on SmolVLA (0% SR, Appendix C) but do not discuss token merging as a conceptually different approach. Since your core claim is that \"deletion is the failure mechanism,\" have you considered whether merging (which preserves information via averaging) could succeed where pruning fails on entangling connectors? This distinction is important for the \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" framework.\n63\t\n64\t3. **On the double dissociation**: The attention-based scoring results on X-VLA are striking (99.2% at k=0.5). Have you tested attention-based scoring on OpenVLA and SmolVLA? If attention scoring works on X-VLA's entangling connector but fails on SmolVLA's entangling connector, the dissociation is more nuanced than \"entangling vs. independent.\"\n65\t\n66\t4. **On real-world relevance**: The practical recommendation is to \"compute CV before deploying token pruning.\" In practice, deploying VLAs involves real-time constraints where latency matters. Can you provide wall-clock measurements showing that the proposed Diagnose-then-Compress pipeline actually delivers meaningful speedups on real inference hardware? The 6% latency improvement reported for VTR on SmolVLA (154ms vs. 164ms) seems insufficient to justify the added complexity.\n67\t\n68\t5. **On the RoPE hypothesis**: You attribute pi0.5's random pruning cliff partly to RoPE position embedding disruption (Appendix A). This is a testable hypothesis -- have you tried pruning without re-indexing (i.e., keeping original position IDs with gaps)? This would directly test whether positional disruption or information loss drives the cliff.\n69\t\n70\t---\n71\t\n72\t## 5. Novelty Assessment\n73\t\n74\t**Is this sufficiently novel for CoRL?**\n75\t\n76\tThe paper occupies an awkward middle ground. As a pure empirical study, it is comprehensive but the core observations (different architectures behave differently; harder tasks are more sensitive) are not deeply surprising. As a methods paper, VTR is a straightforward cross-attention module that only works on one connector type (pixel-shuffle), limiting its generality. The CV diagnostic is practical but validated on too few models for strong claims.\n77\t\n78\tThe double dissociation between scoring method and connector type (Section 4.3, Table 5) is the most genuinely novel finding -- it is non-obvious, well-validated across four suites, and has immediate practical implications. If the paper were restructured to lead with this finding and provide deeper mechanistic analysis, it would be stronger.\n79\t\n80\tThe honest reporting of framework failures and negative results (VTR fails on MLP projectors; the original 2-axis taxonomy broke) adds scientific value but also reveals that the paper's theoretical contribution is weaker than initially conceived. The surviving findings are solid empirical observations rather than a predictive theory.\n81\t\n82\t**Comparison with concurrent work**: The cross-architecture comparison is genuinely the first of its kind -- VLA-IAP, EfficientVLA, LightVLA, and VLA-Pruner all validate on single architectures. This breadth is the paper's strongest positioning advantage. However, the concurrent works propose novel pruning methods with demonstrated speedups, while this paper's method contribution (VTR) is limited. The paper is better positioned as a diagnostic/analysis contribution than as a methods contribution.\n83\t\n84\t**Verdict**: The paper provides useful empirical knowledge that the CoRL community would benefit from, particularly the pixel-shuffle catastrophic failure and the scoring-connector dissociation. However, the novelty bar for a top venue requires either (a) deeper mechanistic insight than correlational observations, (b) a validated predictive framework rather than a post-hoc taxonomy, or (c) a practical method with broad applicability. The paper partially achieves (a) and (b) but falls short on generality and causal rigor.\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## 6. Minor Issues\n89\t\n90\t- The abstract mentions \"6 VLA models\" but the actual closed-loop evaluation covers only 4 (pi0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA, SmolVLA). pi0 and OFT are offline-only. This inflates the scope.\n91\t- Table 2 (main results) uses seed 42 throughout except where noted, but the OpenVLA random column mixes seed 7 and seed 42 data at different retention ratios (acknowledged in appendix but confusing in main text).\n92\t- The VTR baseline discrepancy (44.8% LoRA reproduction vs. 59.2% official checkpoint) is acknowledged but creates ambiguity about which baseline VTR is compared against. Table 8 in the main text uses 44.8% as the baseline, while the dream report uses 59.2%.\n93\t- Some notation inconsistency: \"pp\" (percentage points) is used throughout but the distinction from percentage is not always clear, especially when both absolute and relative changes appear.\n94\t- The paper mentions RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler) in the setup section but this model does not appear in the main results table or the actual paper draft sections. It only appears in the dream report version.\n95\t- Several references are incomplete or use placeholder formatting (e.g., \"VLA-IAP Team,\" \"Compressor-VLA Team,\" \"LightVLA Team\"). These need proper bibliographic entries.\n96\t- The \"Diagnose-then-Compress framework\" is presented as a contribution but is essentially a decision tree (if CV < X do Y). Calling this a \"framework\" overstates the contribution.\n97\t\n98\t---\n99\t\n100\t## 7. Scores\n101\t\n102\t| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n103\t|-----------|-------|---------------|\n104\t| **Soundness** | 3/4 | Experiments are thorough with multi-seed validation and appropriate statistical tests. Main weakness: causal claims exceed correlational evidence, and CV validation has n=3 closed-loop models. |\n105\t| **Presentation** | 3/4 | Well-organized with clear tables and honest limitations. The paper is dense with 11+ tables but readable. The abstract slightly oversells scope (6 models vs. 4 closed-loop). Some \"--\" entries in key tables reduce impact. |\n106\t| **Contribution** | 2/4 | The cross-architecture comparison is the first of its kind, and the scoring-connector dissociation is genuinely novel. However, the core observations (architectures differ, hard tasks are harder) are expected. CV diagnostic is undertested. VTR has narrow applicability. The \"2-axis framework\" is more of a post-hoc taxonomy than a predictive theory. |\n107\t| **Overall Score** | 5/10 | Marginally below acceptance. The paper provides useful empirical knowledge and the cross-architecture breadth is genuine value-add. The scoring-connector dissociation and pixel-shuffle catastrophic failure are findings the community needs. However, the novelty is primarily observational rather than mechanistic, the CV diagnostic is undertested (n=3), LIBERO-only evaluation limits significance for CoRL, and the paper's theoretical contribution (2-axis framework) was weakened when cross-suite data broke the original formulation. With stronger mechanistic analysis, real-robot validation, or broader CV validation, this could be a solid accept. |\n108\t| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I have read the paper carefully including all appendices and supplementary materials. I am familiar with the VLA pruning literature and the LIBERO benchmark. My main uncertainty is whether the CoRL community would value the breadth of the empirical study enough to offset the limited novelty in method and theory. |\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## Summary Assessment\n113\t\n114\tThis is a thorough empirical study that asks an important question: why does token pruning work on some VLA architectures but not others? The cross-architecture evaluation is genuinely the first of its kind, and several findings (pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff, scoring-connector double dissociation, VTR connector-dependent effectiveness) are both novel and practically relevant. However, the paper's theoretical contribution is weaker than its framing suggests -- the \"2-axis framework\" is essentially a post-hoc taxonomy with limited predictive power, validated on too few models. The sim-only evaluation, missing data in key tables, and narrow applicability of VTR further limit the contribution. The paper would benefit from either (a) deeper mechanistic analysis that goes beyond correlational observations, (b) broader model coverage for the CV diagnostic, or (c) real-robot demonstration. As submitted, it sits at the boundary between a strong workshop paper and a marginal conference paper for CoRL.\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvndC3Dc1jbppiGwAPPi2c","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"3fa7034e-31c1-4736-9f32-317012226960","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:25:39.614Z"}
