{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ar7G3TzTP4LAJ7uXfHYcqM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n11\t\\usepackage{array}\n12\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\etal}{\\textit{et~al.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\cf}{\\textit{cf.}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n21\t\\newcommand{\\vs}{\\textit{vs.}}\n22\t\n23\t\\title{Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others:\\\\The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility}\n24\t\n25\t\\author{\n26\tAnonymous Authors\\\\\n27\tConference on Robot Learning (CoRL) 2026 Submission\n28\t}\n29\t\n30\t\\date{}\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{document}\n33\t\\maketitle\n34\t\n35\t% ============================================================\n36\t\\begin{abstract}\n37\t% ============================================================\n38\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models process hundreds of vision tokens per frame, creating a computational bottleneck for real-time robot control. A growing body of work proposes inference-time token pruning to reduce this cost, yet pruning succeeds on some VLA architectures while catastrophically failing on others. We present the first systematic study explaining this variance. Across 6 VLA models (450M--7B parameters), 4 connector families, and 4 LIBERO task suites, we find that token pruning robustness is governed by two interacting axes: (1)~\\emph{token coupling}, determined by the vision connector architecture (token-independent \\vs\\ token-entangling), and (2)~\\emph{task complexity}, which amplifies pruning damage on entangling connectors. We introduce importance coefficient of variation (CV) as a zero-cost, single-forward-pass diagnostic that forms a continuous spectrum (0.038$\\to$0.314) predicting both pruning tolerance and the optimal pruning strategy. Importance-based pruning is actively harmful on low-CV architectures ($+$36\\pp\\ gap favoring random selection), while it helps on high-CV models. For architectures where all deletion-based methods fail, we show that Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR) achieves near-lossless compression at any ratio (0--87.5\\%), recovering from 0\\% success rate under hard pruning to baseline-level performance ($\\approx$59\\%). VTR's effectiveness is itself connector-dependent---it rescues pixel-shuffle entanglement but fails on MLP projectors---validating the diagnostic framework. We additionally demonstrate that attention-based importance metrics rescue entangling connectors where norm-based metrics fail, achieving 99.2\\% success rate at 50\\% compression on X-VLA (versus 42.6\\% with L2-norm). Together, these findings constitute a Diagnose-then-Compress framework: measure CV to predict behavior, select the appropriate pruning strategy or re-encoding method, and compress.\n39\t\\end{abstract}\n40\t\n41\t% ============================================================\n42\t\\section{Introduction}\n43\t\\label{sec:intro}\n44\t% ============================================================\n45\t\n46\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models unify perception and control by feeding visual tokens directly into a language-model backbone, enabling instruction-following robotic manipulation. However, the vision encoder typically produces 50--768 tokens per image, dominating the key-value cache and attention computation during autoregressive action decoding. This motivates inference-time token reduction: if uninformative vision tokens can be identified and removed, latency and memory drop proportionally.\n47\t\n48\tRecent methods---FastV, VLA-IAP, Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA, and See What Matters---propose various pruning strategies for VLA vision tokens. These methods report success on specific architectures, yet no work has systematically examined \\emph{why} pruning succeeds on some VLAs and fails on others. The implicit assumption that token pruning behavior generalizes across architectures is untested and, as we show, incorrect.\n49\t\n50\tWe identify the \\emph{vision connector}---the module that maps vision-encoder outputs to the language-model input space---as the critical architectural factor. Across 6 VLA models spanning 4 connector families (pure linear projection, multi-layer MLP, BART encoder, and pixel-shuffle), we discover that pruning robustness is governed by two interacting axes:\n51\t\n52\t\\begin{itemize}\n53\t\\item \\textbf{Axis~1: Token Coupling.} Connectors that preserve per-token spatial independence (\\eg, linear projection, MLP) yield \\emph{token-independent} representations where pruning damage is local and graded. Connectors that mix information across tokens (\\eg, BART encoder, pixel-shuffle) yield \\emph{token-entangling} representations where removing any token cascades into global information loss.\n54\t\n55\t\\item \\textbf{Axis~2: Task Complexity.} Multi-step tasks (goal-conditioned, long-horizon) amplify pruning damage on entangling connectors. X-VLA drops 27.4\\pp\\ from Object to Goal at the same keep ratio $k\\!=\\!0.9$, while token-independent $\\pi$0.5 drops only 7.6\\pp.\n56\t\\end{itemize}\n57\t\n58\tWe introduce the \\emph{importance coefficient of variation} (CV) as a zero-cost diagnostic for Axis~1 position. CV forms a continuous spectrum from 0.038 ($\\pi$0, most independent) to 0.314 (SmolVLA, most entangled) that predicts both pruning tolerance and the optimal pruning strategy: L2-norm-based selection is actively harmful on low-CV models ($+$36\\pp\\ gap favoring random at $k\\!=\\!0.5$ on $\\pi$0.5), while it helps on high-CV models ($+$5.4\\pp\\ on OpenVLA). This scaling law holds at multiple keep ratios with $|\\rho|\\!=\\!1.0$ (Spearman).\n59\t\n60\tFor architectures where deletion fails entirely (SmolVLA pixel-shuffle: 0\\% at 5\\% removal), we propose Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M params) that achieves near-lossless compression at any ratio. VTR's effectiveness is itself connector-dependent: it rescues pixel-shuffle entanglement (0\\%$\\to$60.1\\%) but fails on MLP projectors (0\\% SR), further validating the diagnostic framework. We also show that attention-based importance metrics rescue entangling connectors where norm-based metrics fail (X-VLA: 99.2\\% \\vs\\ 42.6\\% at $k\\!=\\!0.5$).\n61\t\n62\tOur contributions:\n63\t\\begin{enumerate}\n64\t\\item A 2-axis framework (token coupling $\\times$ task complexity) explaining VLA pruning robustness across 6 models, 4 connector families, and 4 task suites.\n65\t\\item Importance CV as a zero-cost continuous diagnostic predicting pruning tolerance and optimal strategy.\n66\t\\item Evidence that pixel-shuffle creates irreversible token entanglement, collapsing to 0\\% SR at 5\\% removal.\n67\t\\item VTR for near-lossless compression (0--87.5\\%) where deletion fails, with demonstrated connector-dependency.\n68\t\\item Attention-based pruning as the correct importance metric for entangling connectors ($+$56.6\\pp\\ over L2-norm).\n69\t\\end{enumerate}\n70\t\n71\t\n72\t% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n74\t\\label{sec:background}\n75\t% ============================================================\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n78\tVLAs condition a pretrained language model on vision tokens to predict robot actions. Architectures differ primarily in how vision-encoder outputs are mapped to the LLM input space---the \\emph{vision connector}. We study four connector families:\n79\t\\begin{itemize}\n80\t\\item \\textbf{Pure linear projection}: $\\pi$0 (3.3B) and $\\pi$0.5 (4B) use a linear layer (with optional $\\sqrt{d}$ scaling) to project SigLIP patches directly into the LLM embedding space.\n81\t\\item \\textbf{Multi-layer MLP}: OpenVLA (7B) and OpenVLA-OFT (7B) use a 3-layer MLP with GELU activations. OFT fuses DINOv2 and SigLIP features but uses the same MLP structure.\n82\t\\item \\textbf{Linear + BART encoder}: X-VLA (880M) linearly projects patches then passes them through a 12-layer BART encoder, introducing substantial cross-token mixing.\n83\t\\item \\textbf{Pixel-shuffle}: SmolVLA (450M) spatially folds 1024 SigLIP patches into 128 tokens via channel-to-spatial reorganization, creating extreme cross-token entanglement.\n84\t\\end{itemize}\n85\t\n86\tAdditionally, we evaluate a \\textbf{Perceiver Resampler} connector (RoboFlamingo) that uses 64 learned query tokens attending to all vision patches via cross-attention, providing a fourth connector family.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Token Pruning for VLMs/VLAs.}\n89\tFastV, SparseVLM, and See What Matters prune vision tokens based on attention weights in vision-language models. VLA-IAP and LightVLA extend this to robot control, reporting success on MLP-connector VLAs. Compressor-VLA and SpecPrune-VLA propose learned compression. None systematically compares pruning behavior across connector architectures or explains why pruning fails on certain VLAs.\n90\t\n91\t\n92\t% ============================================================\n93\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n94\t\\label{sec:setup}\n95\t% ============================================================\n96\t\n97\t\\paragraph{Models.} We evaluate 6 VLA models spanning 4 model sizes and 4 connector families: $\\pi$0 (3.3B, pure linear, 256 tokens), $\\pi$0.5 (4B, pure linear with $\\sqrt{d}$ scaling, 768 tokens), X-VLA (880M, linear + 12-layer BART, 50 tokens), OpenVLA (7B, 3-layer MLP, 256 tokens), OpenVLA-OFT (7B, 3-layer MLP with DINOv2+SigLIP fusion, 512 tokens), SmolVLA (450M, pixel-shuffle, 128 tokens), and RoboFlamingo (Perceiver Resampler, 64 tokens).\n98\t\n99\t\\paragraph{Benchmark.} All models are evaluated on LIBERO, a simulated tabletop manipulation benchmark comprising four task suites of increasing complexity:\n100\t\\begin{itemize}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvndC3Dc1jbppiGwAPPi2c","session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"3e39f075-0260-40e2-9ac5-38fd5ea49513","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:25:53.742Z"}
