{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvndC3Dc1jbppiGwAPPi2c","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The consolidated AC review report has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key outcomes:\n\n**Report filed at**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T050917/best/review_report.md`\n\n**Final Recommendation**: 5.5/10 — Borderline (Weak Reject)\n\n**Key findings from the roundtable synthesis**:\n\n- **Factual corrections applied**: Both R1 and R2 incorrectly criticized \"single pruning ratio\" — the paper evaluates 6+ k values per model. This error was flagged and should not affect scoring.\n\n- **Consensus strengths**: Cross-architecture breadth (first of its kind), pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff (definitive negative result), double dissociation between scoring method and connector type (most novel finding), and honest limitation reporting.\n\n- **Consensus weaknesses**: CV diagnostic validated on only 3 closed-loop models (n=3 \"scaling law\"), incomplete data matrix with \"--\" entries, LIBERO-only simulation evaluation, confounded cross-model comparisons, narrow/derivative VTR contribution, and no efficiency measurements.\n\n- **Contribution divergence resolved**: R1 (3/4) valued practical breadth; R2 (2/4) emphasized limited methodological novelty. AC consolidated at 2.5/4 — acknowledging genuine empirical novelty but limited theoretical/methodological advance.\n\n- **Actionable rebuttal priorities**: (1) Complete the data matrix, (2) demonstrate CV predicts beyond connector family membership, (3) add efficiency benchmarks, (4) test attention-based pruning on additional models, (5) address VTR batch-size confound."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a3ec3c0f7c2dceba9 (use SendMessage with to: 'a3ec3c0f7c2dceba9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 88636\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 190847</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"02aa251b-7edb-4ffe-933e-bb51fce214bc","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:28:31.169Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are the Area Chair (AC) for a CoRL 2026 submission, conducting Roundtable Steps 2-4: cross-sharing, convergence, and final consolidated report.\n\n## Paper\n\"A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures\"\n- 5 VLA models × 4 LIBERO suites × 3 pruning methods\n- 7 pages, 11 tables, no figures\n\n## Important Context for AC\n\nBefore synthesizing, note these factual corrections regarding both reviews:\n\n**Both R1 and R2 incorrectly criticize \"single pruning ratio.\"** The paper actually evaluates MULTIPLE pruning ratios:\n- Table 3: π0.5 at k=0.30, 0.50, 0.70, 0.85, 0.90, 0.925, 0.95 (7 values)\n- Table 5: X-VLA at k=0.50, 0.70, 0.80, 0.85, 0.90 (5 values)\n- Table 2: π0.5 at k=0.9 across 4 suites\n- SmolVLA: tested at k=0.95 and k=0.50\n\nThis is a significant reviewer error — the paper does provide pruning ratio sweeps showing degradation curves, cliff zones, and method preference reversals across k.\n\n**R2 incorrectly says \"no figures.\"** While the PDF indeed has no figures (only tables), this is a presentation choice, not a completeness issue. However, the 11-table-no-figure format IS legitimately dense.\n\n**The \"2-axis framework\" is NOT the central contribution.** The paper explicitly reports it fails (Table 10, 62% accuracy) and reframes the contribution as an empirical study. Table 11 (Claim Survival) is an act of intellectual honesty, not a central claim. Both reviewers treat the failed framework as if the paper is claiming it works — the paper is doing the opposite.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Score: 6/10\n\n### Strengths:\n- Breadth of experimental matrix (5×4×3 = 60 primary configs)\n- Pixel-shuffle entanglement finding is concrete and actionable\n- Importance metric vs connector architecture finding (56.6pp gap) is strong\n- Per-suite LIBERO breakdown reveals meaningful task-dependent variation\n- Reproducibility effort (standardized benchmark, clear protocol)\n\n### Weaknesses:\n- Predictive framework underdeveloped (62% accuracy) — but see AC note: paper explicitly reports failure\n- No latency/throughput/memory measurements\n- \"Single pruning ratio\" — FACTUAL ERROR, paper has multiple k values\n- LIBERO-only evaluation limits generalizability\n- No recovery fine-tuning experiments\n- Statistical reporting thin (mostly single-seed except VTR and X-VLA cliff)\n\n### Questions:\n1. Pruning ratio sensitivity? (answered by Tables 3, 5, 6)\n2. Framework details and validation?\n3. Recovery fine-tuning after pruning?\n4. Efficiency measurements?\n5. VTR mechanism analysis?\n\n### Missing experiments:\n- Inference efficiency benchmarks\n- Recovery fine-tuning\n- Additional benchmark beyond LIBERO\n- Layer-wise pruning analysis\n- Combination strategies\n\n### Scores: Soundness 2/4, Presentation 3/4, Contribution 3/4, Overall 6/10, Confidence 4/5\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Score: 5/10\n\n### Strengths:\n- Breadth of experimental coverage (most comprehensive cross-arch comparison)\n- Pixel-shuffle entanglement as failure mode is most novel contribution\n- Importance metric > architecture is striking\n- Honest reporting of framework failures\n- VTR recovery analysis is practically useful\n\n### Weaknesses:\n- Central framework fails (62%) — but see AC note: paper frames this as honest negative result\n- Limited novelty over concurrent works (VLA-IAP, EfficientVLA, LightVLA all published/in review)\n- No figures in 7-page paper with 11 tables\n- \"Single pruning ratio\" — FACTUAL ERROR\n- LIBERO-only evaluation\n- CV scaling law failure underexplored\n\n### Novelty Assessment: \"Incremental to low\" — breadth-of-evaluation contribution, not methodological advance. Pixel-shuffle finding and metric>architecture are novel but not developed into solutions.\n\n### Scores: Soundness 2/4, Presentation 2/4, Contribution 2/4, Overall 5/10, Confidence 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task: Roundtable Steps 2-4\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Share Summary\nIdentify what R1 and R2 agree on and where they diverge.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n- Flag the reviewer factual errors (pruning ratio criticism)\n- Determine whether each weakness withstands cross-examination\n- Identify which concerns are addressable in rebuttal vs. fundamental\n- Weigh R2's novelty concern against R1's more favorable view of practical contribution\n\n### Step 4: Consolidated Report\nWrite the FINAL review report with:\n\n1. **Paper Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Agreed Strengths** (what both reviewers affirm)\n3. **Agreed Weaknesses** (what both identify, after correcting errors)\n4. **Contested Points** (where reviewers diverge, with AC resolution)\n5. **Factual Corrections** (reviewer errors that should not affect scoring)\n6. **Key Questions for Rebuttal** (most important 3-5)\n7. **Missing Elements** (what would strengthen the paper)\n8. **Limitations Assessment** (how honestly does the paper report limitations?)\n9. **CoRL Relevance** (is this appropriate for the robot learning community?)\n10. **Final Scores**:\n    - Soundness (1-4)\n    - Presentation (1-4)\n    - Contribution (1-4)\n    - Overall (1-10, with justification referencing CoRL acceptance threshold of ~25-33%)\n    - Confidence (1-5)\n11. **Recommendation** (accept/borderline/reject, with 1-2 sentence justification)\n\nBe fair and calibrated. The paper has real strengths (unprecedented cross-architecture VLA pruning comparison) and real weaknesses (no efficiency metrics, LIBERO-only). Weigh these against CoRL standards.\n","agentId":"a3ec3c0f7c2dceba9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The consolidated AC review report has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key outcomes:\n\n**Report filed at**: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T050917/best/review_report.md`\n\n**Final Recommendation**: 5.5/10 — Borderline (Weak Reject)\n\n**Key findings from the roundtable synthesis**:\n\n- **Factual corrections applied**: Both R1 and R2 incorrectly criticized \"single pruning ratio\" — the paper evaluates 6+ k values per model. This error was flagged and should not affect scoring.\n\n- **Consensus strengths**: Cross-architecture breadth (first of its kind), pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff (definitive negative result), double dissociation between scoring method and connector type (most novel finding), and honest limitation reporting.\n\n- **Consensus weaknesses**: CV diagnostic validated on only 3 closed-loop models (n=3 \"scaling law\"), incomplete data matrix with \"--\" entries, LIBERO-only simulation evaluation, confounded cross-model comparisons, narrow/derivative VTR contribution, and no efficiency measurements.\n\n- **Contribution divergence resolved**: R1 (3/4) valued practical breadth; R2 (2/4) emphasized limited methodological novelty. AC consolidated at 2.5/4 — acknowledging genuine empirical novelty but limited theoretical/methodological advance.\n\n- **Actionable rebuttal priorities**: (1) Complete the data matrix, (2) demonstrate CV predicts beyond connector family membership, (3) add efficiency benchmarks, (4) test attention-based pruning on additional models, (5) address VTR batch-size confound."}],"totalDurationMs":190847,"totalTokens":88636,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4120,"cache_read_input_tokens":84101,"output_tokens":414,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4120},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":414,"cache_read_input_tokens":84101,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_i