{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rc2kmdNRG8jSxYsUoqMzu4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive context. The \"best\" tier dream report already contains a thorough consolidated review. Let me now produce the complete, final AC consolidated review report incorporating all details from the actual paper, the project context, and the review findings.\n\n---\n\n# Area Chair Consolidated Review Report\n\n**Paper**: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: Vision Connectors and VLA Token Compressibility\"\n**Venue**: CoRL 2026\n**Submission ID**: [Redacted]\n**Roundtable Protocol**: Step 2 (Cross-Share) + Step 3 (Convergence) + Step 4 (AC Final Report)\n**R1 Initial Score**: 6/10 (marginally above) | **R2 Initial Score**: 5/10 (marginally below)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of vision token pruning across six VLA architectures (pi0 3.3B, pi0.5 4B, X-VLA 880M, OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B, SmolVLA 450M), spanning three vision connector families (linear projection, MLP projector, token-entangling: BART encoder and pixel-shuffle), evaluated on four LIBERO task suites (Object, Spatial, Goal, Long) of increasing manipulation complexity. Three pruning strategies are tested: L2-norm selection, random subsampling, and attention-based scoring, at retention ratios k in {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95} with additional fine-grained values for specific models. Four of six models are evaluated in closed-loop robotic control; two (pi0, OFT) contribute offline metrics only.\n\nThe central claim is a two-axis framework: pruning robustness is jointly determined by (1) token coupling imposed by the vision connector (token-independent vs. token-entangling) and (2) task complexity, which amplifies pruning damage on entangling connectors. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms is proposed as a single-pass, zero-cost diagnostic that predicts both pruning tolerance class and optimal scoring strategy. CV spans a continuous spectrum from 0.038 (pi0, most tolerant) to 0.314 (SmolVLA, catastrophic). A scoring-method double dissociation is identified: attention-based scoring is effective on X-VLA's entangling connector (+31pp over random at k=0.5) but harmful on pi0.5's linear connector (-2 to -30pp), while L2-norm shows the converse. For pixel-shuffle architectures where all deletion strategies fail (0% SR even at k=0.95), the authors introduce Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module (~22M parameters) that recovers SmolVLA from 0% to over 60% SR at 75% compression, with a flat M-curve showing near-lossless performance at any compression ratio from 0% to 87.5%.\n\nThe paper contains 11 tables, multiple figures (architecture diagram, degradation curves, cross-suite comparison, token heatmap, hypergeometric cliff model, entropy layers), and an extensive appendix with per-task breakdowns, multi-seed validation, confounder analysis, and mechanistic discussion.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Agreed Strengths\n\nBoth reviewers affirm the following after cross-examination:\n\n**S1. Unprecedented cross-architecture breadth.** Six VLA models across three connector families, four LIBERO task suites, three pruning methods, and six or more retention ratios per model. No concurrent work -- SP-VLA, VLA-IAP, LightVLA, EfficientVLA, Compressor-VLA, or \"See What Matters\" -- evaluates pruning across more than one or two architectures. This breadth is the paper's primary differentiator and fills a genuine gap in the rapidly growing VLA efficiency literature. The factorial design (connector x task x method x k) enables interaction effects that single-architecture studies cannot detect.\n\n**S2. Pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff is definitive.** SmolVLA collapses to 0% SR (or at most 6.6% on Spatial at k=0.95) across all four LIBERO suites, all pruning methods (L2-norm, random, attention, ToMe, ACIS, Sobel geometric prior), all retention ratios including k=0.95 (removing only 6 of 128 tokens), both checkpoints (LoRA-reproduced at 44.8% baseline and official HuggingFace at 59.2%), and dual seeds. The mechanism is clearly explained: pixel-shuffle 4x4 folding creates non-overlapping receptive fields with zero inter-token redundancy, making every token simultaneously essential. This is a rigorous, mechanistically interpretable, and practically important negative result that the community needs.\n\n**S3. Double dissociation between scoring method and connector type.** The paper's strongest finding. Attention-based scoring achieves 99.2% on X-VLA Object at k=0.5 while L2-norm achieves only 42.6% (a 56.6pp gap). Conversely, on pi0.5, L2-norm outperforms random by up to 93pp at k=0.85 on Object, while attention is neutral on three suites and catastrophically harmful on Object (-30.4pp). This structural interaction is genuinely novel, non-obvious, validated across multiple suites and retention ratios, and has immediate practical implications. It goes well beyond \"different models behave differently.\"\n\n**S4. Intellectual honesty and transparent limitation reporting.** Both reviewers commend the paper's unusual candor. The confounder appendix explicitly acknowledges that six architectures differ simultaneously in backbone, encoder, scale, and connector, preventing causal isolation. The VTR failure on OpenVLA (0% SR) is reported rather than suppressed. The CV diagnostic's small sample size (n=3-4 closed-loop) is flagged. The paper refrains from claiming VTR \"exceeds baseline,\" instead framing it as \"recovery from 0% to near-baseline.\" This level of intellectual honesty is rare and scientifically valuable.\n\n**S5. Thorough multi-seed validation for key claims.** The SmolVLA cliff (dual-seed, dual-checkpoint), pi0.5 cliff zone (seed42/43, sub-1pp std at k=0.5), X-VLA cliff localization (3-seed, std=0.3pp at k=0.85), X-VLA random vs. L2 crossover (seed42/43, delta=0.4pp at k=0.5), and VTR M-curve (3-seed for all M values) are all multi-seed validated. Cross-seed deltas are small (typically under 5pp), confirming that the major phenomena are architectural rather than stochastic.\n\n**S6. Non-monotonic phenomena carefully documented.** The pi0.5 random cliff at k=0.85-0.925 (74% to 5% then recovery to 74% at k=0.7), the X-VLA k=0.85/k=0.80 inversion (21pp gap from 2 tokens), and the pi0.5 L2-norm V-shaped curve (valley at k=0.5, recovery at k=0.3) are all multi-seed confirmed and given mechanistic accounts. The hypergeometric cliff model (C=106, T=7, RMSE=0.018) provides a quantitative framework for the pi0.5 random cliff, and the token position analysis (single cluster at k=0.5 with 78% coverage vs. 100% coverage at k=0.7) directly confirms the spatial clustering hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Agreed Weaknesses\n\nBoth reviewers identify these weaknesses, which withstand cross-examination and correction of the \"single pruning ratio\" factual error:\n\n**W1. CV diagnostic is statistically underpowered.** The \"scaling law\" (Table in Appendix) and \"continuous spectrum\" claims rest on three closed-loop data points for the strategy-prediction claim (pi0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA). SmolVLA contributes nothing to the strategy comparison (both methods = 0%). A perfect monotonic ordering with n=3 has probability 1/6 under the null, barely significant. The paper uses phrases like \"scaling law\" and \"continuous predictor\" that overstate what three data points can support. OFT and pi0 contribute offline metrics only (CV measurement), padding the model count to six without adding closed-loop validation. Within the MLP connector family, OpenVLA (CV=0.215) and OFT (CV=0.219) differ by only 0.004 in CV, but OFT has no closed-loop evaluation to test whether this minuscule CV difference corresponds to a measurable pruning robustness difference. CV may be a proxy for connector type membership rather than an independent predictor.\n\n**W2. Incomplete data matrix undermines \"systematic\" claim.** The main results table (Table 3) has multiple \"--\" entries, particularly for OpenVLA (only Object suite in most conditions; Goal/Spatial/Long missing or partial). X-VLA Spatial k=0.7, X-VLA Long k=0.7, and several other cells are absent. For a paper claiming to be \"the first systematic cross-architecture study,\" the structural gaps in the actual data matrix are notable. Some experiments appear to have still been running at submission time (the ROADMAP shows active GPU jobs for OpenVLA Random Goal/Spatial and Long experiments).\n\n**W3. LIBERO-only evaluation with no sim-to-real evidence.** All results come from a single simulation benchmark on one robot platform. The abandoned SimplerEnv attempt (OpenVLA baseline = 0% due to ManiSkill3 integration issues) and weak MetaWorld results (SmolVLA baseline 26.4%, all pruned conditions collapsed) suggest the findings' transferability is uncertain. For CoRL, where real-robot or at least multi-simulator validation is increasingly expected (acceptance rate 25-33%), the absence of any non-LIBERO validation is a meaningful gap. LIBERO baselines also vary widely across models (SmolVLA 59.2% vs. X-VLA 99.2%), meaning models with higher baselines have more headroom for graceful degradation, potentially confounding cross-model comparisons.\n\n**W4. Confounded cross-model comparisons.** The six models differ simultaneously in LLM backbone (PaliGemma for pi0/pi0.5, Llama-2 for OpenVLA, Qwen2 for SmolVLA, BART decoder for X-VLA), vision encoder (SigLIP variants, DINOv2+SigLIP for OFT), scale (450M to 7B), training data, and connector architecture. Only the OFT vs. OpenVLA pair provides a semi-controlled comparison (same connector type, same backbone, different action head), but OFT has no closed-loop evaluation due to Blackwell GPU SDPA incompatibility. The paper's language often implies connector is causally isolated (\"connector determines both compressibility AND optimal strategy\") when the evidence is strictly correlational. The pi0-OpenVLA pair (same 256 SigLIP tokens, different connector) is the closest available control but also differs in backbone and fusion strategy.\n\n**W5. VTR contribution is narrow and architecturally derivative.** VTR is structurally identical to Perceiver Resampler (Flamingo) and Q-Former (BLIP-2) -- the paper itself acknowledges this (\"learned re-encoding,\" \"structurally similar to BLIP-2 Q-Former\"). It is validated on SmolVLA only; application to OpenVLA (MLP projector) yielded 0% SR with severe overfitting (identical action every timestep at 20K steps). The paper attributes this to architectural specificity (\"VTR specifically solves pixel-shuffle entanglement\"), but the investigation is insufficient: no alternative learning rates, no training loss curves, no frozen-backbone ablation, no diagnosis of whether failure is fundamental or procedural. The VTR training protocol uses batch size 4 on 1 GPU versus the official batch size 512 on 8 GPUs, producing a weakened LoRA baseline (44.8% vs. 59.2% official). While the paper correctly reframes around the official baseline for context, the VTR's own training conditions remain mismatched.\n\n**W6. No efficiency measurements for an efficiency-motivated paper.** The abstract and introduction motivate the work by \"computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control\" and \"token pruning offers a training-free solution,\" yet the paper contains no systematic latency, throughput, or memory benchmarks. The only efficiency figure is a brief mention of 6% latency improvement for VTR in the appendix (154.78ms vs. 164.33ms) and \"75% KV-cache memory reduction\" stated without measurement details. For a paper whose primary practical motivation is inference efficiency, the absence of wall-clock measurements across models and retention ratios is a notable omission.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Contested Points (AC Resolution)\n\n**4a. Contribution novelty (R1: 3/4 vs. R2: 2/4).**\n\nR1 values the practical, actionable contribution: the cross-architecture comparison fills a genuine gap in the literature, the double dissociation has immediate practical implications for VLA deployment, and the pixel-shuffle cliff is a result practitioners need. R2 emphasizes that the individual components lack methodological novelty: CV = sigma/mu of L2 norms is a trivial statistic, attention-based scoring is not new, VTR is a known architecture (Perceiver Resampler) applied in a new context, and the 2-axis framework is descriptive rather than predictive -- it organizes observations but does not predict cliff locations, amplification magnitudes, or exact thresholds.\n\n**AC Resolution**: The cross-architecture breadth and double dissociation are genuine contributions that exceed \"running existing methods on more models\" -- the interaction effects (scoring x connector, task x connector) could not have been discovered by any single-architecture study, and the direction of these interactions (which scoring method helps which connector) was not predictable a priori. However, the 2-axis framework is indeed more descriptive than predictive: it classifies architectures post-hoc but does not tell a practitioner what will happen to a new VLA with CV=0.18 on a new benchmark. The \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" pipeline is essentially a decision tree (measure CV; if low, use L2; if entangling, use attention; if pixel-shuffle, use VTR), not a trained or formally validated predictor. **Consolidated: 2.5/4** -- acknowledging real empirical novelty (the findings are novel even if the tools are not) but limited methodological and theoretical advance.\n\n**4b. Paper framing and the 2-axis framework.**\n\nThe task description notes that the paper's own framework prediction accuracy (62%) is reported as intellectual honesty, not as a central claim. After reading the submitted paper, I find that the 2-axis framework is listed as Contribution #1 in the introduction and presented positively throughout Section 4.1 and the conclusion. The framework's limitations (predictions breaking down across suites, e.g., pi0.5 random k=0.9 on Object=9.0% vs. Goal=62.2%) are documented in the appendix but not flagged as framework failures in the main text. The 62% accuracy figure appears in the project framing documents (paper_framing.md) but not in the submitted paper itself. Both reviewers are therefore justified in engaging with the framework as a positive claim. The paper would benefit from more explicit main-text discussion of where the framework's predictions break down.\n\n**AC Resolution**: Reviewers' treatment of the framework as a positive claim is appropriate given how the paper presents it. The honest acknowledgment in project documents is commendable but does not substitute for main-text qualification.\n\n**4c. \"Single pruning ratio\" criticism (both R1 and R2).**\n\nBoth reviewers initially criticized the paper for evaluating \"only a single pruning ratio.\" This is factually incorrect. The paper evaluates k in {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95} as standard, with additional values (0.925, 0.8, 0.2, 0.1) for specific models. Tables 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, and multiple appendix tables show full degradation curves. The cliff zone characterization (pi0.5 k=0.85-0.925, X-VLA k=0.85/0.80) explicitly requires fine-grained k sweeps.\n\n**AC Resolution**: This criticism is factually invalid and is struck from the record. Its correction does not change the core concerns (CV underpowered, sim-only, VTR narrow) but does remove one listed weakness per reviewer, improving the soundness assessment by approximately 0.25 points.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Factual Corrections\n\n1. **\"Single pruning ratio\" (attributed to both R1 and R2)**: Incorrect. The paper evaluates six or more retention ratios per model, with degradation curves spanning k=0.1 to k=0.95. Multiple tables (main results, cross-suite, scaling law, cliff localization) show multiple k values. Full eight-point curves exist for X-VLA (k=0.1 through 0.95) and six-point curves for OpenVLA.\n\n2. **\"No figures\" (R2 initial impression)**: The submitted paper includes Figure 1 (hero overview/teaser), Figure 2 (pruning degradation overview), Figure 3 (pixel-shuffle architecture diagram), Figure 4 (method gap visualization), plus appendix figures (cross-suite comparison, token heatmap, hypergeometric model, entropy layers). The paper is table-dense (11 tables) but not figure-free. R2's concern about visual density is legitimate -- the ratio of tables to figures is high and the paper would benefit from consolidating some tabular data into degradation curve plots.\n\n3. **\"SmolVLA collapses at k=0.5\"**: Several points in the paper clarify that SmolVLA collapses at k=0.95 (5% removal), not just at aggressive pruning. The cliff is at the very first tested retention ratio.\n\n4. **Neither reviewer's factual errors change the overall assessment by more than ~0.5 points.** The pruning ratio error, once corrected, removes one listed weakness per reviewer but does not address the substantive concerns about CV validation, sim-only evaluation, VTR scope, or data completeness.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Questions for Rebuttal\n\n**Q1. CV beyond connector type membership.** Can you demonstrate that CV is predictive beyond what connector family already tells us? Within the MLP family (OpenVLA CV=0.215 vs. OFT CV=0.219, delta=0.004), does the CV difference correspond to a measurable pruning robustness difference? If not, CV is a discrete classifier (3 bins: <0.1, 0.1-0.25, >0.3) rather than a continuous predictor. What happens for a hypothetical VLA with CV=0.18 -- does the framework predict threshold-graded or graded behavior? Without this, the \"continuous spectrum\" framing is unsubstantiated.\n\n**Q2. Attention-based scoring on additional models.** The double dissociation is the paper's strongest finding, but the attention x connector table has only two entries: X-VLA (attention effective) and pi0.5 (attention harmful on Object, neutral elsewhere). OpenVLA attention scoring is absent from the submitted paper. Does attention help or hurt on OpenVLA (MLP, CV=0.215)? Completing this cell would either strengthen the dissociation pattern (if attention is neutral/slightly harmful on MLP, confirming the independent-connector pattern) or complicate it (if attention helps, blurring the connector-type boundary). Either outcome is informative.\n\n**Q3. Efficiency measurements.** Can you provide wall-clock inference latency and peak GPU memory measurements for at least two models (e.g., pi0.5 at 768 tokens and OpenVLA at 256 tokens) at two retention ratios (e.g., k=0.5 and k=0.9)? A paper motivating token pruning by inference efficiency must quantify the efficiency gains, not just the accuracy costs. The 6% VTR speedup suggests the latency impact is modest for small models; does it become significant for 7B models?\n\n**Q4. VTR batch size confound.** VTR trained at batch size 4 yields a LoRA baseline of 44.8%, while the official SmolVLA checkpoint achieves 59.2% at batch size 512. VTR's recovery to ~60% SR could partly reflect the reduced training quality of the baseline rather than genuine re-encoding benefit. The M=128 passthrough achieving 58.0% (from 44.8% baseline) is presented as evidence that re-encoding itself helps, but this could also reflect the additional 100K training steps at different hyperparameters. The +100K step control (45.0%) partially addresses this, but the control uses the same small batch size. How confident are you that VTR at official batch size 512 would still exceed baseline?\n\n**Q5. Position re-indexing confound.** After token pruning, remaining tokens receive contiguous new position indices. The paper acknowledges that this interacts with RoPE in pi0.5 (Appendix A). Have you tested a zero-masking ablation (replacing pruned tokens with zero vectors while preserving original position indices) to isolate information loss from positional disruption? SmolVLA's existing zero-mask results show 0% (Table 7), suggesting information loss dominates for pixel-shuffle. But for pi0.5's cliff zone, positional disruption may be the primary mechanism rather than information loss per se.\n\n**Q6. X-VLA CV reconciliation.** X-VLA (CV=0.151, classified as \"entangling\") has lower CV than OpenVLA (CV=0.215, classified as \"independent\"). If CV measures entanglement, entangling connectors should produce more heterogeneous tokens and higher CV. How do you reconcile the ordering? Is CV measuring \"importance heterogeneity\" (as stated) rather than \"entanglement\" per se? If so, the interpretive framework conflates two distinct concepts.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Missing Elements\n\nRanked by expected impact on the paper's strength if addressed:\n\n1. **Complete data matrix.** Fill all \"--\" entries in the main results table, especially OpenVLA cross-suite (Goal, Spatial, Long) and X-VLA Long k=0.7. The paper claims \"systematic study\" but the actual matrix has structural gaps. The ROADMAP shows these experiments were in progress at submission time; if results are available, include them.\n\n2. **Efficiency benchmarks.** Latency (ms per action), peak GPU memory (MB), and throughput (Hz) at representative k values for at least two models. Without this, the practical motivation (real-time robotic control efficiency) is aspirational rather than demonstrated.\n\n3. **Attention-based scoring on OpenVLA.** This would complete the double dissociation table's MLP column. Even a single k value (k=0.5 on Object) would be informative.\n\n4. **Head-to-head comparison with one concurrent method.** Running VLA-IAP or SP-VLA on the same LIBERO setup (even on one model) would demonstrate the diagnostic framework's explanatory power: does the concurrent method's behavior match the paper's predictions based on connector type?\n\n5. **Position-preserving ablation (zero-mask control).** The paper already has SmolVLA zero-mask data (0%, confirming information loss dominates). A pi0.5 zero-mask experiment would isolate whether the random cliff is driven by information loss or positional disruption.\n\n6. **Additional benchmark or real-robot validation.** Even one model on one non-LIBERO benchmark with reasonable baselines would strengthen generalizability claims. The SimplerEnv and MetaWorld attempts both failed to produce usable baselines, which is understandable but leaves the single-benchmark limitation unaddressed.\n\n7. **Alternative diagnostic metrics.** Comparing CV against kurtosis, effective rank, or pairwise cosine similarity of post-connector tokens would demonstrate that CV is the best available diagnostic rather than merely a diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Limitations Assessment\n\nThe paper's limitation reporting is above average for the field. Specific acknowledgments:\n\n- **Simulation-only evaluation**: Explicitly stated in the Limitations paragraph (Section 4.5) and extended in Appendix A.\n- **Confounder analysis**: Appendix F provides a dedicated section discussing backbone, encoder, scale, and training data confounds. The language correctly notes \"preventing causal isolation\" and \"limiting causal interpretation.\"\n- **CV sample size**: The introduction's contribution statement includes the hedging \"validated on four closed-loop architectures; we expect the trend to generalize but acknowledge the limited sample.\"\n- **VTR scope**: The failure on OpenVLA is reported, and the paper states \"VTR is validated on SmolVLA only.\"\n- **Baseline discrepancy**: The gap between LoRA-reproduced (44.8%) and official (59.2%) SmolVLA baselines is noted, with VTR framed as \"recovery from 0%\" rather than \"exceeding baseline.\"\n- **Single-seed caveat**: \"Most conditions use a single seed; cross-seed validation shows delta less than 5.8pp.\"\n\n**Assessment**: The internal project documents (paper_framing.md, memory.md) contain even more candid self-assessment -- abandoned SimplerEnv, failed MetaWorld transfers, framework breakage under cross-suite data (the 62% framework accuracy figure) -- but this deeper self-awareness does not fully surface in the submitted paper. The paper could be more explicit in the main text about: (a) the CV diagnostic's statistical limitation (n=3 for strategy prediction), (b) the data matrix gaps as opposed to merely using \"--\" notation, and (c) the 2-axis framework's prediction failures across suites. Limitations reporting is honest but the formatting choice to place most caveats in the appendix means a reader of the main text alone may overestimate the strength of the claims.\n\n---\n\n## 9. CoRL Relevance\n\nToken pruning for VLA inference efficiency is directly relevant to robot learning, addressing a real deployment bottleneck as VLA models scale. The question \"why does pruning work on some VLAs but not others?\" is important and timely: at least five concurrent works (SP-VLA, VLA-IAP, LightVLA, EfficientVLA, Compressor-VLA) propose VLA-specific pruning methods, each validated on a single architecture. This paper's cross-architecture perspective provides the context these single-architecture works lack.\n\nLIBERO is a respected benchmark in the CoRL community, used by the official evaluations of several models studied here. The practical recommendations (measure CV before deploying pruning; use attention scoring on entangling connectors; use L2-norm on independent connectors; avoid hard deletion on pixel-shuffle) are immediately actionable for practitioners.\n\n**However**, CoRL increasingly expects real-robot validation or at least multi-simulator evaluation (the acceptance rate of 25-33% selects for papers with deployment evidence). A pure LIBERO simulation study, even an exceptionally thorough one, may be seen as better suited to an ML venue (NeurIPS, ICML) where empirical analysis contributions are more valued and the sim-only constraint is less penalized. The paper's strongest practical claim -- that connector architecture determines pruning robustness and optimal strategy -- has clear implications for VLA design, which is squarely CoRL territory, but the evidence base is simulation-only.\n\n**Verdict**: Topically appropriate for CoRL. The sim-only limitation is a more significant gap at CoRL than it would be at NeurIPS or ICML. The paper's analysis contribution is stronger than its method contribution, which slightly misaligns with CoRL's traditional emphasis on methods and systems.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 (initial) | R2 (initial) | AC Consolidated | Justification |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|-----------------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 | 2/4 | **3/4** | Experiments are thorough where data exists, with multi-seed validation, appropriate controls (M=128 passthrough, MLP adapter parameter-matched control, extended training control), and Wilson CIs. AC upgrades from R1/R2 because the \"single pruning ratio\" criticism was a factual error, and the overall experimental methodology is rigorous. Main soundness gaps: causal claims exceed correlational evidence (connector effect not isolated from backbone/scale), CV diagnostic has n=3 for strategy prediction (trivially achievable monotonic ordering), and data matrix has structural gaps. The hypergeometric cliff model fits 2 free parameters to 3 data points (effectively interpolation, not validated on held-out ratios). |\n| **Presentation** | 3/4 | 2/4 | **2.5/4** | Well-organized paper with clear methodology section, explicit pruning protocol, and logical flow from framework to evidence to method. However: 11 tables creates extreme density; some key evidence is relegated to appendix that readers may not reach; multiple \"--\" entries in key tables without clear indication of whether data is missing by design or by incomplete experimentation; abstract and introduction claims \"six architectures\" but only four have closed-loop evaluation, which could mislead readers; \"scaling law\" framing for 3 data points is an overclaim. The paper would benefit from consolidating some tabular data into degradation curve figures and adding a visual summary (scatter plot of CV vs. strategy gap). |\n| **Contribution** | 3/4 | 2/4 | **2.5/4** | The cross-architecture breadth is a genuine first-of-its-kind contribution: no concurrent work covers more than 1-2 architectures. The double dissociation between scoring method and connector type is novel, well-evidenced, and practically valuable -- this is the paper's strongest finding. The pixel-shuffle cliff documentation is rigorous and practically important. However: CV is an elementary statistic validated on too few models; VTR is structurally derivative (Perceiver Resampler) and validated on one model; the 2-axis framework is descriptive (classifies post-hoc) rather than predictive (forecasts new architectures). The paper's contribution is empirical synthesis and cataloguing, not methodological or theoretical advance. |\n| **Overall** | 6/10 | 5/10 | **5.5/10** | See detailed justification below. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | 4/5 | **4/5** | Thorough review of the complete paper (main text, all appendix sections), project context documents (paper_framing.md, memory.md, ROADMAP.md), and experimental records. Familiar with the VLA pruning literature (VLA-IAP, SP-VLA, LightVLA, EfficientVLA, Compressor-VLA), the LIBERO benchmark, and the VLA architectures studied. The remaining 1/5 uncertainty reflects the possibility that ongoing experiments (OpenVLA cross-suite, pi0.5 Long cliff zone) could materially change the assessment. |\n\n**Overall Justification (5.5/10)**: The paper addresses a timely and important question with the most comprehensive cross-architecture VLA pruning evaluation to date. The double dissociation finding (attention helps entangling connectors, hurts independent ones; L2-norm shows the converse) is genuinely novel and has immediate practical value. The pixel-shuffle cliff is a definitive negative result. The intellectual honesty throughout is commendable.\n\nHowever, the paper falls short of CoRL acceptance (estimated 25-33% rate) on several axes simultaneously:\n- The CV \"scaling law\" is a 3-point correlation that could be a proxy for connector type membership rather than an independent predictor\n- The data matrix has structural gaps in the main results, undermining the \"systematic\" claim\n- There are no efficiency measurements for a paper motivated by inference efficiency\n- VTR is narrowly validated (1 model, fails on the only other model tested) and architecturally derivative\n- The evaluation is simulation-only on a single benchmark\n- The 2-axis framework is descriptive (organizes existing observations) rather than predictive (forecasts new architectures or benchmarks)\n\nNo single weakness is fatal, but their combination places the paper below the acceptance threshold. The paper is a strong workshop paper or a borderline conference paper. It contributes useful empirical knowledge that the community needs, but the theoretical and methodological contributions do not meet the bar for a top-25% CoRL paper.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Recommendation\n\n**Borderline -- Weak Reject (5.5/10).**\n\nThe paper provides genuinely useful empirical knowledge and its cross-architecture breadth is a real differentiator from concurrent VLA pruning works. The double dissociation and pixel-shuffle cliff are findings the community needs, and the intellectual honesty throughout is exemplary. However, the combination of underpowered CV validation, structural data gaps, absent efficiency benchmarks, narrow VTR scope, simulation-only evaluation, and a descriptive (not predictive) theoretical framework collectively place it below the CoRL acceptance threshold.\n\n**What would move this to accept**: (a) Complete the OpenVLA cross-suite data and fill all \"--\" entries; (b) add attention-based scoring on OpenVLA to complete the dissociation table; (c) validate CV on 2-3 additional VLA architectures (even offline CV measurements from published models would help); (d) provide wall-clock efficiency measurements; (e) any non-LIBERO validation with reasonable baselines. Addressing items (a), (b), and (d) -- all achievable within a revision cycle -- would likely move the paper to a solid accept.\n\n**What would move this to reject**: (a) If additional VLA models break the monotonic CV ordering, undermining the diagnostic's validity; (b) if OpenVLA cross-suite data reveals that MLP projectors also cliff on complex tasks (breaking the 2-axis framework); (c) if VTR failure on OpenVLA is shown to be a training protocol issue rather than architectural, undermining the \"connector-dependent re-encoding\" narrative.\n\n**Suggested venue alternatives if rejected**: NeurIPS 2026 (empirical analysis track), ICML 2026 (where sim-only evaluation is less penalized), or RSS 2027 (if real-robot validation can be added). The paper's analysis contribution is well-suited to ML venues; the CoRL fit depends on addressing the deployment evidence gap."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aabebb919629d018e (use SendMessage with to: 'aabebb919629d018e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 108182\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 264978</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47b17614-1ae9-46bc-87bd-87205f677c45","uuid":"26e811f7-abfd-4f2f-84fb-4165654780b4","timestamp":"2026-05-25T06:33:13.864Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are the Area Chair who just completed a roundtable review of the CoRL 2026 submission \"A Systematic Study of Token Pruning Strategies Across VLA Architectures.\"\n\nPlease provide your COMPLETE consolidated review report. Do not abbreviate any section. Include all 11 sections in full:\n\n1. Paper Summary\n2. Agreed Strengths\n3. Agreed Weaknesses\n4. Contested Points (with AC resolution)\n5. Factual Corrections\n6. Key Questions for Rebuttal\n7. Missing Elements\n8. Limitations Assessment\n9. CoRL Relevance\n10. Final Scores (Soundness, Presentation, Contribution, Overall, Confidence)\n11. Recommendation\n\nContext reminder:\n- R1 scored 6/10 (marginally above), R2 scored 5/10 (marginally below)\n- Both reviewers incorrectly criticized \"single pruning ratio\" — the paper has multiple k values\n- The paper explicitly reports its own framework failure (62% accuracy) as intellectual honesty, not as a central claim\n- Key surviving findings: pixel-shuffle entanglement (0% SR), importance metric > connector (56.6pp gap), VTR architecture-specific (0%→59% SmolVLA, 0% OpenVLA)\n- No latency/efficiency measurements, LIBERO-only evaluation, no figures (11 tables)\n- CoRL acceptance rate ~25-33%\n\nPaper details:\n- 5 VLA models (π0, π0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA, SmolVLA), 3 connector families, 4 LIBERO suites\n- Pruning methods: L2-norm, random, attention-based\n- VTR M-sweep: M=16 to M=128, 3-seed, baseline 59.2%\n- Cross-suite data shows framework predictions break down (π0.5 random k=0.9: Object=9.3% vs Goal=62.2%)\n- X-VLA attention pruning at k=0.5: 99.2% vs L2norm 42.6%\n- OpenVLA VTR: all configs fail (0-5% SR), LoRA baseline also 0%\n\nYour final recommendation was ~5.5 (borderline / weak reject). Please reproduce the full report.\n","agentId":"aabebb919629d018e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive context. The \"best\" tier dream report already contains a thorough consolidated review. Let me now produce the complete, final AC consolidated review report incorporating all details from the actual paper, the project context, and the review findings.\n\n---\n\n# Area Chair Consolidated Review Report\n\n**Paper**: \"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: Vision Connectors and VLA Token Compressibility\"\n**Venue**: CoRL 2026\n**Submission ID**: [Redacted]\n**Roundtable Protocol**: Step 2 (Cross-Share) + Step 3 (Convergence) + Step 4 (AC Final Report)\n**R1 Initial Score**: 6/10 (marginally above) | **R2 Initial Score**: 5/10 (marginally below)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic empirical study of vision token pruning across six VLA architectures (pi0 3.3B, pi0.5 4B, X-VLA 880M, OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B, SmolVLA 450M), spanning three vision connector families (linear projection, MLP projector, token-entangling: BART encoder and pixel-shuffle), evaluated on four LIBERO task suites (Object, Spatial, Goal, Long) of increasing manipulation complexity. Three pruning strategies are tested: L2-norm selection, random subsampling, and attention-based scoring, at retention ratios k in {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95} with additional fine-grained values for specific models. Four of six models are evaluated in closed-loop robotic control; two (pi0, OFT) contribute offline metrics only.\n\nThe central claim is a two-axis framework: pruning robustness is jointly determined by (1) token coupling imposed by the vision connector (token-independent vs. token-entangling) and (2) task complexity, which amplifies pruning damage on entangling connectors. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms is proposed as a single-pass, zero-cost diagnostic that predicts both pruning tolerance class and optimal scoring strategy. CV spans a continuous spectrum from 0.038 (pi0, most tolerant) to 0.314 (SmolVLA, catastrophic). A scoring-method double dissociation is identified: attention-based scoring is effective on X-VLA's entangling connector (+31pp over random at k=0.5) but harmful on pi0.5's linear connector (-2 to -30pp), while L2-norm shows the converse. For pixel-shuffle architectures where all deletion strategies fail (0% SR even at k=0.95), the authors introduce Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module (~22M parameters) that recovers SmolVLA from 0% to over 60% SR at 75% compression, with a flat M-curve showing near-lossless performance at any compression ratio from 0% to 87.5%.\n\nThe paper contains 11 tables, multiple figures (architecture diagram, degradation curves, cross-suite comparison, token heatmap, hypergeometric cliff model, entropy layers), and an extensive appendix with per-task breakdowns, multi-seed validation, confounder analysis, and mechanistic discussion.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Agreed Strengths\n\nBoth reviewers affirm the following after cross-examination:\n\n**S1. Unprecedented cross-architecture breadth.** Six VLA models across three connector families, four LIBERO task suites, three pruning methods, and six or more retention ratios per model. No concurrent work -- SP-VLA, VLA-IAP, LightVLA, EfficientVLA, Compressor-VLA, or \"See What Matters\" -- evaluates pruning across more than one or two architectures. This breadth is the paper's primary differentiator and fills a genuine gap in the rapidly growing VLA efficiency literature. The factorial design (connector x task x method x k) enables interaction effects that single-architecture studies cannot detect.\n\n**S2. Pixel-shuffle catastrophic cliff is definitive.** SmolVLA collapses to 0% SR (or at most 6.6% on Spatial at k=0.95) across all four LIBERO suites, all pruning methods (L2-norm, random, attention, ToMe, ACIS, Sobel geometric prior), all retention ratios including k=0.95 (removing only 6 of 128 tokens), both checkpoints (LoRA-reproduced at 44.8% baseline and official HuggingFace at 59.2%), and dual seeds. The mechanism is clearly explained: pixel-shuffle 4x4 folding creates non-overlapping receptive fields with zero inter-token redundancy, making every token simultaneously essential. This is a rigorous, mechanistically interpretable, and practically important negative result that the community needs.\n\n**S3. Double dissociation between scoring method and connector type.** The paper's strongest finding. Attention-based scoring achieves 99.2% on X-VLA Object at k=0.5 while L2-norm achieves only 42.6% (a 56.6pp gap). Conversely, on pi0.5, L2-norm outperforms random by up to 93pp at k=0.85 on Object, while attention is neutral on three suites and catastrophically harmful on Object (-30.4pp). This structural interaction is genuinely novel, non-obvious, validated across multiple suites and retention ratios, and has immediate practical implications. It goes well beyond \"different models behave differently.\"\n\n**S4. Intellectual honesty and transparent limitation reporting.** Both reviewers commend the paper's unusual candor. The confounder appendix explicitly acknowledges that six architectures differ simultaneously in backbone, encoder, scale, and connector, preventing causal isolation. The VTR failure on OpenVLA (0% SR) is reported rather than suppressed. The CV diagnostic's small sample size (n=3-4 closed-loop) is flagged. The paper refrains from claiming VTR \"exceeds baseline,\" instead framing it as \"recovery from 0% to near-baseline.\" This level of intellectual honesty is rare and scientifically valuable.\n\n**S5. Thorough multi-seed validation for key claims.** The SmolVLA cliff (dual-seed, dual-checkpoint), pi0.5 cliff zone (seed42/43, sub-1pp std at k=0.5), X-VLA cliff localization (3-seed, std=0.3pp at k=0.85), X-VLA random vs. L2 crossover (seed42/43, delta=0.4pp at k=0.5), and VTR M-curve (3-seed for all M values) are all multi-seed validated. Cross-seed deltas are small (typically under 5pp), confirming that the major phenomena are architectural rather than stochastic.\n\n**S6. Non-monotonic phenomena carefully documented.** The pi0.5 random cliff at k=0.85-0.925 (74% to 5% then recovery to 74% at k=0.7), the X-VLA k=0.85/k=0.80 inversion (21pp gap from 2 tokens), and the pi0.5 L2-norm V-shaped curve (valley at k=0.5, recovery at k=0.3) are all multi-seed confirmed and given mechanistic accounts. The hypergeometric cliff model (C=106, T=7, RMSE=0.018) provides a quantitative framework for the pi0.5 random cliff, and the token position analysis (single cluster at k=0.5 with 78% coverage vs. 100% coverage at k=0.7) directly confirms the spatial clustering hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Agreed Weaknesses\n\nBoth reviewers identify these weaknesses, which withstand cross-examination and correction of the \"single pruning ratio\" factual error:\n\n**W1. CV diagnostic is statistically underpowered.** The \"scaling law\" (Table in Appendix) and \"continuous spectrum\" claims rest on three closed-loop data points for the strategy-prediction claim (pi0.5, X-VLA, OpenVLA). SmolVLA contributes nothing to the strategy comparison (both methods = 0%). A perfect monotonic ordering with n=3 has probability 1/6 under the null, barely significant. The paper uses phrases like \"scaling law\" and \"continuous predictor\" that overstate what three data points can support. OFT and pi0 contribute offline metrics only (CV measurement), padding the model count to six without adding closed-loop validation. Within the MLP connector family, OpenVLA (CV=0.215) and OFT (CV=0.219) differ by only 0.004 in CV, but OFT has no closed-loop evaluation to test whether this minuscule CV difference corresponds to a measurable pruning robustness difference. CV may be a proxy for connector type membership rather than an independent predictor.\n\n**W2. Incomplete data matrix undermines \"systematic\" claim.** The main results table (Table 3) has multiple \"--\" entries, particularly for OpenVLA (only Object suite in most conditions; Goal/Spatial/Long missing or partial). X-VLA Spatial k=0.7, X-VLA Long k=0.7, and several other cells are absent. For a paper claiming to be \"the first systematic cross-architecture study,\" the structural gaps in the actual data matrix are notable. Some experiments appear to have still been running at submission time (the ROADMAP shows active GPU jobs for OpenVLA Random Goal/Spatial and Long experiments).\n\n**W3. LIBERO-only evaluation with no sim-to-real evidence.** All results come from a single simulation benchmark on one robot platform. The abandoned SimplerEnv attempt (OpenVLA baseline = 0% due to ManiSkill3 integration issues) and weak MetaWorld results (SmolVLA baseline 26.4%, all pruned conditions collapsed) suggest the findings' transferability is uncertain. For CoRL, where real-robot or at least multi-simulator validation is increasingly expected (acceptance rate 25-33%), the absence of any non-LIBERO validation is a meaningful gap. LIBERO baselines also vary widely across models (SmolVLA 59.2% vs. X-VLA 99.2%), meaning models with higher baselines have more headroom for graceful degradation, potentially confounding cross-model comparisons.\n\n**W4. Confounded cross-model comparisons.** The six models differ simultaneously in LLM backbone (PaliGemma for pi0/pi0.5, Llama-2 for OpenVLA, Qwen2 for SmolVLA, BART decoder for X-VLA), vision encoder (SigLIP variants, DINOv2+SigLIP for OFT), scale (450M to 7B), training data, and connector architecture. Only the OFT vs. OpenVLA pair provides a semi-controlled comparison (same connector type, same backbone, different action head), but OFT has no closed-loop evaluation due to Blackwell GPU SDPA incompatibility. The paper's language often implies connector is causally isolated (\"connector determines both compressibility AND optimal strategy\") when the evidence is strictly correlational. The pi0-OpenVLA pair (same 256 SigLIP tokens, different connector) is the closest available control but also differs in backbone and fusion strategy.\n\n**W5. VTR contribution is narrow and architecturally derivative.** VTR is structurally identical to Perceiver Resampler (Flamingo) and Q-Former (BLIP-2) -- the paper itself acknowledges this (\"learned re-encoding,\" \"structurally similar to BLIP-2 Q-Former\"). It is validated on SmolVLA only; application to OpenVLA (MLP projector) yielded 0% SR with severe overfitting (identical action every timestep at 20K steps). The paper attributes this to architectural specificity (\"VTR specifically solves pixel-shuffle entanglement\"), but the investigation is insufficient: no alternative learning rates, no training loss curves, no frozen-backbone ablation, no diagnosis of whether failure is fundamental or procedural. The VTR training protocol uses batch size 4 on 1 GPU versus the official batch size 512 on 8 GPUs, producing a weakened LoRA baseline (44.8% vs. 59.2% official). While the paper correctly reframes around the official baseline for context, the VTR's own training conditions remain mismatched.\n\n**W6. No efficiency measurements for an efficiency-motivated paper.** The abstract and introduction motivate the work by \"computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control\" and \"token pruning offers a training-free solution,\" yet the paper contains no systematic latency, throughput, or memory benchmarks. The only efficiency figure is a brief mention of 6% latency improvement for VTR in the appendix (154.78ms vs. 164.33ms) and \"75% KV-cache memory reduction\" stated without measurement details. For a paper whose primary practical motivation is inference efficiency, the absence of wall-clock measurements across models and retention ratios is a notable omission.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Contested Points (AC Resolution)\n\n**4a. Contribution novelty (R1: 3/4 vs. R2: 2/4).**\n\nR1 values the practical, actionable contribution: the cross-architecture comparison fills a genuine gap in the literature, the double dissociation has immediate practical implications for VLA deployment, and the pixel-shuffle cliff is a result practitioners need. R2 emphasizes that the individual components lack methodological novelty: CV = sigma/mu of L2 norms is a trivial statistic, attention-based scoring is not new, VTR is a known architecture (Perceiver Resampler) applied in a new context, and the 2-axis framework is descriptive rather than predictive -- it organizes observations but does not predict cliff locations, amplification magnitudes, or exact thresholds.\n\n**AC Resolution**: The cross-architecture breadth and double dissociation are genuine contributions that exceed \"running existing methods on more models\" -- the interaction effects (scoring x connector, task x connector) could not have been discovered by any single-architecture study, and the direction of these interactions (which scoring method helps which connector) was not predictable a priori. However, the 2-axis framework is indeed more descriptive than predictive: it classifies architectures post-hoc but does not tell a practitioner what will happen to a new VLA with CV=0.18 on a new benchmark. The \"Diagnose-then-Compress\" pipeline is essentially a decision tree (measure CV; if low, use L2; if entangling, use attention; if pixel-shuffle, use VTR), not a trained or formally validated predictor. **Consolidated: 2.5/4** -- acknowledging real empirical novelty (the findings are novel even if the tools are not) but limited methodological and theoretical advance.\n\n**4b. Paper framing and the 2-axis framework.**\n\nThe task description notes that the paper's own framework prediction accuracy (62%) is reported as intellectual honesty, not as a central claim. After reading the submitted paper, I find that the 2-axis framework is listed as Contribution #1 in the introduction and presented positively throughout Section 4.1 and the conclusion. The framework's limitations (predictions breaking down across suites, e.g., pi0.5 random k=0.9 on Object=9.0% vs. Goal=62.2%) are documented in the appendix but not flagged as framework failures in the main text. The 62% accuracy figure appears in the project framing documents (paper_framing.md) but not in the submitted paper itself. Both reviewers are therefore justified in engaging with the framework as a positive claim. The paper would benefit from more explicit main-text discussion of where the framework's predictions break down.\n\n**AC Resolution**: Reviewers' treatment of the framework as a positive claim is appropriate given how the paper presents it. The honest acknowledgment in project documents is commendable but does not substitute for main-text qualification.\n\n**4c. \"Single pruning ratio\" criticism (both R1 and R2).**\n\nBoth reviewers initially criticized the paper for evaluating \"only a single pruning ratio.\" This is factually incorrect. The paper evaluates k in {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95} as standard, with additional values (0.925, 0.8, 0.2, 0.1) for specific models. Tables 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, and multiple appendix tables show full degradation curves. The cliff zone characterization (pi0.5 k=0.85-0.925, X-VLA k=0.85/0.80) explicitly requires fine-grained k sweeps.\n\n**AC Resolution**: This criticism is factually invalid and is struck from the record. Its correction does not change the core concerns (CV underpowered, sim-only, VTR narrow) but does remove one listed weakness per reviewer, improving the soundness assessment by approximately 0.25 points.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Factual Corrections\n\n1. **\"Single pruning ratio\" (attributed to both R1 and R2)**: Incorrect. The paper evaluates six or more retention ratios per model, with degradation curves spanning k=0.1 to k=0.95. Multiple tables (main results, cross-suite, scaling law, cliff localization) show multiple k values. Full eight-point curves exist for X-VLA (k=0.1 through 0.95) and six-point curves for OpenVLA.\n\n2. **\"No figures\" (R2 initial impression)**: The submitted paper includes Figure 1 (hero overview/teaser), Figure 2 (pruning degradation overview), Figure 3 (pixel-shuffle architecture diagram), Figure 4 (method gap visualization), plus appendix figures (cross-suite comparison, token heatmap, hypergeometric model, entropy layers). The paper is table-dense (11 tables) but not figure-free. R2's concern about visual density is legitimate -- the ratio of tables to figures is high and the paper would benefit from consolidating some tabular data into degradation curve plots.\n\n3. **\"SmolVLA collapses at k=0.5\"**: Several points in the paper clarify that SmolVLA collapses at k=0.95 (5% removal), not just at aggressive pruning. The cliff is at the very first tested retention ratio.\n\n4. **Neither reviewer's factual errors change the overall assessment by more than ~0.5 points.** The pruning ratio error, once corrected, removes one listed weakness per reviewer but does not address the substantive concerns about CV validation, sim-only evaluation, VTR scope, or data completeness.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Questions for Rebuttal\n\n**Q1. CV beyond connector type membership.** Can you demonstrate that CV is predictive beyond what connector family already tells us? Within the MLP family (OpenVLA CV=0.215 vs. OFT CV=0.219, delta=0.004), does the CV difference correspond to a measurable pruning robustness difference? If not, CV is a discrete classifier (3 bins: <0.1, 0.1-0.25, >0.3) rather than a continuous predictor. What happens for a hypothetical VLA with CV=0.18 -- does the framework predict threshold-graded or graded behavior? Without this, the \"continuous spectrum\" framing is unsubstantiated.\n\n**Q2. Attention-based scoring on additional models.** The double dissociation is the paper's strongest finding, but the attention x connector table has only two entries: X-VLA (attention effective) and pi0.5 (attention harmful on Object, neutral elsewhere). OpenVLA attention scoring is absent from the submitted paper. Does attention help or hurt on OpenVLA (MLP, CV=0.215)? Completing this cell would either strengthen the dissociation pattern (if attention is neutral/slightly harmful on MLP, confirming the independent-connector pattern) or complicate it (if attention helps, blurring the connector-type boundary). Either outcome is informative.\n\n**Q3. Efficiency measurements.** Can you provide wall-clock inference latency and peak GPU memory measurements for at least two models (e.g., pi0.5 at 768 tokens and OpenVLA at 256 tokens) at two retention ratios (e.g., k=0.5 and k=0.9)? A paper motivating token pruning by inference efficiency must quantify the efficiency gains, not just the accuracy costs. The 6% VTR speedup suggests the latency impact is modest for small models; does it become significant for 7B models?\n\n**Q4. VTR batch size confound.** VTR trained at batch size 4 yields a LoRA baseline of 44.8%, while the official SmolVLA checkpoint achieves 59.2% at batch size 512. VTR's recovery to ~60% SR could partly reflect the reduced training quality of the baseline rather than genuine re-encoding benefit. The M=128 passthrough achieving 58.0% (from 44.8% baseline) is presented as evidence that re-encoding itself helps, but this could also reflect the additional 100K training steps at different hyperparameters. The +100K step control (45.0%) partially addresses this, but the control uses the same small batch size. How confident are you that VTR at official batch size 512 would still exceed baseline?\n\n**Q5. Position re-indexing confound.** After token pruning, remaining tokens receive contiguous new position indices. The paper acknowledges that this interacts with RoPE in pi0.5 (Appendix A). Have you tested a zero-masking ablation (replacing pruned tokens with zero vectors while preserving original position indices) to isolate information loss from positional disruption? SmolVLA's existing zero-mask results show 0% (Table 7), suggesting information loss dominates for pixel-shuffle. But for pi0.5's cliff zone, positional disruption may be the primary mechanism rather than information loss per se.\n\n**Q6. X-VLA CV reconciliation.** X-VLA (CV=0.151, classified as \"entangling\") has lower CV than OpenVLA (CV=0.215, classified as \"independent\"). If CV measures entanglement, entangling connectors should produce more heterogeneous tokens and higher CV. How do you reconcile the ordering? Is CV measuring \"importance heterogeneity\" (as stated) rather than \"entanglement\" per se? If so, the interpretive framework conflates two distinct concepts.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Missing Elements\n\nRanked by expected impact on the paper's strength if addressed:\n\n1. **Complete data matrix.** Fill all \"--\" entries in the main results table, especially OpenVLA cross-suite (Goal, Spatial, Long) and X-VLA Long k=0.7. The paper claims \"systematic study\" but the actual matrix has structural gaps. The ROADMAP shows these experiments were in progress at submission time; if results are available, include them.\n\n2. **Efficiency benchmarks.** Latency (ms per action), peak GPU memory (MB), and throughput (Hz) at representative k values for at least two models. Without this, the practical motivation (real-time robotic control efficiency) is aspirational rather than demonstrated.\n\n3. **Attention-based scoring on OpenVLA.** This would complete the double dissociation table's MLP column. Even a single k value (k=0.5 on Object) would be informative.\n\n4. **Head-to-head comparison with one concurrent method.** Running VLA-IAP or SP-VLA on the same LIBERO setup (even on one model) would demonstrate the diagnostic framework's explanatory power: does the concurrent method's behavior match the paper's predictions based on connector type?\n\n5. **Position-preserving ablation (zero-mask control).** The paper already has SmolVLA zero-mask data (0%, confirming information loss dominates). A pi0.5 zero-mask experiment would isolate whether the random cliff is driven by information loss or positional disruption.\n\n6. **Additional benchmark or real-robot validation.** Even one model on one non-LIBERO benchmark with reasonable baselines would strengthen generalizability claims. The SimplerEnv and MetaWorld attempts both failed to produce usable baselines, which is understandable but leaves the single-benchmark limitation unaddressed.\n\n7. **Alternative diagnostic metrics.** Comparing CV against kurtosis, effective rank, or pairwise cosine similarity of post-connector tokens would demonstrate that CV is the best available diagnostic rather than merely a diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Limitations Assessment\n\nThe paper's limitation reporting is above average for the field. Specific acknowledgments:\n\n- **Simulation-only evaluation**: Explicitly stated in the Limitations paragraph (Section 4.5) and extended in Appendix A.\n- **Confounder analysis**: Appendix F provides a dedicated section discussing backbone, encoder, scale, and training data confounds. The language correctly notes \"preventing causal isolation\" and \"limiting causal interpretation.\"\n- **CV sample size**: The introduction's contribution statement includes the hedging \"validated on four closed-loop architectures; we expect the trend to generalize but acknowledge the limited sample.\"\n- **VTR scope**: The failure on OpenVLA is reported, and the paper states \"VTR is validated on SmolVLA only.\"\n- **Baseline discrepancy**: The gap between LoRA-reproduced (44.8%) and official (59.2%) SmolVLA baselines is noted, with VTR framed as \"recovery from 0%\" rather than \"exceeding baseline.\"\n- **Single-seed caveat**: \"Most conditions use a single seed; cross-seed validation shows delta less than 5.8pp.\"\n\n**Assessment**: The internal project documents (paper_framing.md, memory.md) contain even more candid self-assessment -- abandoned SimplerEnv, failed MetaWorld transfers, framework breakage under cross-suite data (the 62% framework accuracy figure) -- but this deeper self-awareness does not fully surface in the submitted paper. The paper could be more explicit in the main text about: (a) the CV diagnostic's statistical limitation (n=3 for strategy prediction), (b) the data matrix gaps as opposed to merely using \"--\" notation, and (c) the 2-axis framework's prediction failures across suites. Limitations reporting is honest but the formatting choice to place most caveats in the appendix means a reader of the main text alone may overestimate the strength of the claims.\n\n---\n\n## 9. CoRL Relevance\n\nToken pruning for VLA inference efficiency is directly relevant to robot learning, addressing a real deployment bottleneck as VLA models scale. The question \"why does pruning work on some VLAs but not others?\" is important and timely: at least five concurrent works (SP-VLA, VLA-IAP, LightVLA, EfficientVLA, Compressor-VLA) propose VLA-specific pruning methods, each validated on a single architecture. This paper's cross-architecture perspective provides the context these single-architecture works lack.\n\nLIBERO is a respected benchmark in the CoRL community, used by the official evaluations of several models studied here. The practical recommendations (measure CV before deploying pruning; use attention scoring on entangling connectors; use L2-norm on independent connectors; avoid hard deletion on pixel-shuffle) are immediately actionable for practitioners.\n\n**However**, CoRL increasingly expects real-robot validation or at least multi-simulator evaluation (the acceptance rate of 25-33% selects for papers with deployment evidence). A pure LIBERO simulation study, even an exceptionally thorough one, may be seen as better suited to an ML venue (NeurIPS, ICML) where empirical analysis contributions are more valued and the sim-only constraint is less penalized. The paper's strongest practical claim -- that connector architecture determines pruning robustness and optimal strategy -- has clear implications for VLA design, which is squarely CoRL territory, but the evidence base is simulation-only.\n\n**Verdict**: Topically appropriate for CoRL. The sim-only limitation is a more significant gap at CoRL than it would be at NeurIPS or ICML. The paper's analysis contribution is stronger than its method contribution, which slightly misaligns with CoRL's traditional emphasis on methods and systems.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 (initial) | R2 (initial) | AC Consolidated | Justification |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|-----------------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 | 2/4 | **3/4** | Experiments are thorough where data exists, with multi-seed validation, appropriate controls (M=128 passthrough, MLP adapter parameter-matched control, extended training control), and Wilson CIs. AC upgrades from R1/R2 because the \"single pruning ratio\" criticism was a factual error, and the overall experimental methodology is rigorous. Main soundness gaps: causal claims exceed correlational evidence (connector effect not isolated from backbone/scale), CV diagnostic has n=3 for strategy prediction (trivially achievable monotonic ordering), and data matrix has structural gaps. The hypergeometric cliff model fits 2 free parameters to 3 data points (effectively interpolation, not validated on held-out ratios). |\n| **Presentation** | 3/4 | 2/4 | **2.5/4** | Well-organized paper with clear methodology section, explicit pruning protocol, and logical flow from framework to evidence to method. However: 11 tables creates extreme density; some key evidence is relegated to appendix that readers may not reach; multiple \"--\" entries in key tables without clear indication of whether data is missing by design or by incomplete experimentation; abstract and introduction claims \"six architectures\" but only four have closed-loop evaluation, which could mislead readers; \"scaling law\" framing for 3 data points is an overclaim. The paper would benefit from consolidating some tabular data into degradation curve figures and adding a visual summary (scatter plot of CV vs. strategy gap). |\n| **Contribution** | 3/4 | 2/4 | **2.5/4** | The cross-architecture breadth is a genuine first-of-its-kind contribution: no concurrent work covers more than 1-2 architectures. The double dissociation between scoring method and connector type is novel, well-evidenced, and practically valuable -- this is the paper's strongest finding. The pixel-shuffle cliff documentation is rigorous and practically important. However: CV is an elementary statistic validated on too few models; VTR is structurally derivative (Perceiver Resampler) and validated on one model; the 2-axis framework is descriptive (classifies post-hoc) rather than predictive (forecasts new architectures). The paper's contribution is empirical synthesis and cataloguing, not methodological or theoretical advance. |\n| **Overall** | 6/10 | 5/10 | **5.5/10** | See detailed justification below. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | 4/5 | **4/5** | Thorough review of the complete paper (main text, all appendix sections), project context documents (paper_framing.md, memory.md, ROADMAP.md), and experimental records. Familiar with the VLA pruning literature (VLA-IAP, SP-VLA, LightVLA, EfficientVLA, Compressor-VLA), the LIBERO benchmark, and the VLA architectures studied. The remaining 1/5 uncertainty reflects the possibility that ongoing experiments (OpenVLA cross-suite, pi0.5 Long cliff zone) could materially change the assessment. |\n\n**Overall Justification (5.5/10)**: The paper addresses a timely and important question with the most comprehensive cross-architecture VLA pruning evaluation to date. The double dissociation finding (attention helps entangling connectors, hurts independent ones; L2-norm shows the converse) is genuinely novel and has immediate practical value. The pixel-shuffle cliff is a definitive negative result. The intellectual honesty throughout is commendable.\n\nHowever, the paper falls short of CoRL acceptance (estimated 25-33% rate) on several axes simultaneously:\n- The CV \"scaling law\" is a 3-point correlation that could be a proxy for connector type membership rather than an independent predictor\n- The data matrix has structural gaps in the main results, undermining the \"systematic\" claim\n- There are no efficiency measurements for a paper motivated by inference efficiency\n- VTR is narrowly validated (1 model, fails on the only other model tested) and architecturally derivative\n- The evaluation is simulation-only on a single benchmark\n- The 2-axis framework is descriptive (organizes existing observations) rather than predictive (forecasts new architectures or benchmarks)\n\nNo single weakness is fatal, but their combination places the paper below the acceptance threshold. The paper is a strong workshop paper or a borderline conference paper. It contributes useful empirical knowledge that the community needs, but the theoretical and methodological contributions do not meet the bar for a top-25% CoRL paper.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Recommendation\n\n**Borderline -- Weak Reject (5.5/10).**\n\nThe paper provides genuinely useful empirical knowledge and its cross-architecture breadth is a real differentiator from concurrent VLA pruning works. The double dissociation and pixel-shuffle cliff are findings the community needs, and the intellectual honesty throughout is exemplary. However, the combination of underpowered CV validation, structural data gaps, absent efficiency benchmarks, narrow VTR scope, simulation-only evaluation, and a descriptive (not predictive) theoretical framework collectively place it below the CoRL acceptance threshold.\n\n**What would move this to accept**: (a) Complete the OpenVLA cross-suite data and fill all \"--\" entries; (b) add attention-based scoring on OpenVLA to complete the dissociation table; (c) validate CV on 2-3 additional VLA architectures (even offline CV measurements from published models would help); (d) provide wall-clock efficiency measurements; (e) any non-LIBERO validation with reasonable baselines. Addressing items (a), (b), and (d) -- all achievable within a revision cycle -- would likely move the paper to a solid accept.\n\n**What would move this to reject**: (a) If additional VLA models break the monotonic CV ordering, undermining the diagnostic's validity; (b) if OpenVLA cross-suite data reveals that MLP projectors also cliff on complex tasks (breaking the 2-axis framework); (c) if VTR failure on OpenVLA is shown to be a training protocol issue rather than architectural, undermining the \"connector-dependent re-encoding\" narrative.\n\n**Suggested venue alternatives if rejected**: NeurIPS 2026 (empirical analysis track), ICML 2026 (where sim-only evaluation is less penalized), or RSS 2027 (if real-robot validation can be added). The paper's analysis contribution is well-suited to ML venues; the CoRL fit depends on addressing the deployment evidence gap."}],"totalDurationMs":264978,"totalTokens":108182,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10828,"cache_read_input_tokens":89159,"output_tokens":8194,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10828},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":8194,"cache_read_input_tokens":89159,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10828,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10828,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
